;
;       {_______            {_          {______
;             {__          {_ __               {__
;             {__         {_  {__               {__
;          {__           {__   {__               {__
;      {______          {__     {__              {__
;            {__       {__       {__            {__
;              {_________         {______________		Expansion Unit
;
;      gpu64 -- E1M1 with the doors working. A Quake level you can open.
;
;      WHAT THIS PROGRAM IS EVIDENCE FOR
;
;      gpu64_demo_level.a proves the Pi can put a whole Quake level on the
;      HDMI output and hold a player inside it. It is deliberately the
;      smallest program that does: it walks, and nothing in the level ever
;      changes. This one is the next question, and it is the one the API was
;      frozen to answer -- can a C64 run the GAME?
;
;      A level's game is in its entity lump: E1M1 has 369 records naming
;      fourteen doors, seven secret doors, two platforms, six buttons and the
;      twenty-six trigger volumes that fire them. That lump is already in the
;      Pi's memory, and it does not fit in a C64 -- so this program asks for
;      it one record at a time with LEVEL_ENT ($16), keeps the 55 records it
;      cares about, and from then on the whole of the door logic runs on the
;      6502:
;
;        * a touch test against every mover's own box, expanded the way
;          Quake expands it, and against every trigger volume
;        * target / targetname matching, by the opaque 16-bit ids LEVEL_ENT
;          hands back -- no strings and no strcmp on the C64 at all
;        * an open / wait / close state machine per mover
;        * and two things sent to gpu64 per moving door: SET_POSITION on its
;          node run, and the same displacement in the next frame's CLIP_MOVE
;          block, so the door you can see is the door you cannot walk through
;
;      The second half is the part that could not be faked. A door that moves
;      on screen but not in the collision hull is a painting; CLIP_MOVE's
;      mover list is what makes it a door. Stand in a closing doorway and it
;      stops you.
;
;      WHAT IT IS NOT
;
;      Still not the whole of Quake: no monsters, no weapons, no items, no
;      keys and no counters, so the three buttons of the big room open
;      nothing (their trigger_counter is not implemented) and a locked door
;      stays locked. func_door_secret moves in one stage here rather than
;      Quake's two. Those are the next milestone; the protocol underneath
;      them is what this program is for.
;
;      DRIVING IT
;
;        W / S   forward / back          A / D   turn left / right
;        Q / E   strafe left / right     SHIFT   hold to run
;        @ / ;   forward / back, too     SPACE   jump
;        : / =   strafe left / right, too (fixed: the menu cannot move them)
;        F1 / F3 look up / down          F7      level the view
;        + / -   widen / narrow the field of view (30-110 degrees, 75 at start)
;        C= or B fire                    1-4     pick a weapon
;        <-      pause (RESUME / CONTROLS / MAIN MENU; <- again resumes)
;        H       health cheat: +100, at most 250, for testing levels
;        I       weapons cheat: every weapon, full shells and nails
;
;        Joystick 2: up / down walk, left / right turn, button fires.
;        1351 mouse in port 1: sideways turns, forward / back looks up and
;        down, the left button fires.
;
;        There is no way out: RUN/STOP is ignored, pressed or ghosted, and
;        the machine is reset to leave. Only the PC simulator ends a run,
;        through SIM_EXIT below.
;
;        If the C64 freezes, press RESTORE: row 0 then reads NMI, the
;        address the 6502 was at, and a count of presses. A crash into a
;        BRK prints BRK and its address there and stops, instead of dropping
;        to READY.
;
;      Walk up to the first door of E1M1 and it opens, waits, and closes
;      behind you.
;
;      BEFORE RUNNING IT
;
;      Select quake.reu (tools/pack_levels.py) as the REU image in the RAD
;      menu, exactly as for the level demo.
;
;      READING THE SCREEN
;
;      Rows 2-13 are gpu64_demo_level.a's, unchanged, so a run of this
;      program is comparable with a run of that one. The three new rows:
;
;        MOV      movers found / selected for this frame's CLIP_MOVE /
;                 currently animating / trigger volumes found. D is what one
;                 telemetry redraw costs, in ms.
;        OPEN     how many times a mover has started opening, the last
;                 target id fired, the last node id a SET_POSITION was sent
;                 to, and the error byte from those sends.
;        ENT      entities scanned, LEVEL_ENT answers that failed their own
;                 checksum, and records that did not fit the tables.
;
;      SEL is the number that matters for the protocol: CLIP_MOVE takes at
;      most 16 movers and E1M1 has 29, so this program sends the nearest
;      ones -- which is what Quake's own SV_AreaEdicts does, for the same
;      reason.
;
;        VIEW     the field of view in degrees and the pitch the F keys set,
;                 NODES the length of the level's node run as the refresh
;                 ring found it, FIX how many level nodes it had to repair.
;        RING     errors from the refresh rings; PAL how many times the
;                 palette was found damaged and put back (hex), LAST how
;                 many entries that took; CAM how many times the camera had
;                 stopped being a camera and was made one again.
;
;      KEEPING THE LEVEL INTACT
;
;      Bench run 38 walked E1M1 cleanly for a few seconds, then the view
;      jumped and later the colours went. Both are one lost bus bit away:
;      a flipped CMD_HI makes SET_POSITION a class 0 PAL_SET, and a flipped
;      CMD_LO makes it CREATE_OBJECT, which replaces whatever node it hits
;      -- the camera included. Retained state stays broken until something
;      writes it again, so two refresh rings run one step a frame each: the
;      scene-wide state (view, light, active camera, palette, readback
;      fence), and one level node through LEVEL_NODE ($1C). The palette and
;      the level's nodes live in the level file, not here, which is why
;      those two need the Pi's help -- see docs/state-refresh.md.
;
;      Copyright (c) 2026 Honza Slesinger
;
;      This program is free software: you can redistribute it and/or modify
;      it under the terms of the GNU General Public License as published by
;      the Free Software Foundation, either version 3 of the License, or
;      (at your option) any later version.
;

	.include "gpu64.inc"
	.include "gpu64_quake_actors.inc"

; --- class 1 opcodes this program uses ---------------------------------
OP_SCENE_RESET       = $00
OP_SET_VIEWPORT      = $01
OP_SET_PERSPECTIVE   = $02
OP_SET_LIGHT3D       = $03
OP_SET_BACKGROUND    = $05
OP_LOOP_START        = $06
OP_LOOP_STOP         = $07
OP_SCENE_COMMIT      = $08
OP_LOAD_LEVEL        = $13
OP_LEVEL_STEP        = $14
OP_CLIP_MOVE         = $15
OP_LEVEL_ENT         = $16
OP_LEVEL_PALETTE     = $17
OP_LEVEL_NODE        = $1C
OP_CREATE_OBJECT     = $20
OP_CREATE_CAMERA     = $21
OP_SET_ACTIVE_CAMERA = $23
OP_SET_VISIBLE       = $24
OP_CREATE_SPRITE     = $25
OP_CREATE_LIGHT      = $26
OP_SET_SPRITE        = $27
OP_SET_POINT_LIGHT   = $28
OP_SET_POSITION      = $30
OP_SET_ORIENTATION   = $31
OP_SET_SCALE         = $35
OP_GET_TRANSFORM     = $36
OP_WORLD_TICK        = $6C

KEY_DESTRUCTIVE = $a5
KEY_CHECKED	= $d0		; | n, see stage2
CHK_TRIES	= 4


LEVEL_DONE = $ff

MESH_BASE = 1000
NODE_BASE = 100
CAM_ID    = 1

VP_W  = 320
VP_H  = 200
NEARZ = 26
FARZ  = 32767
READY_WAIT = 20000

CIA2_TALO = $dd04
CIA2_TAHI = $dd05
CIA2_TBLO = $dd06
CIA2_TBHI = $dd07
CIA2_CRA  = $dd0e
CIA2_CRB  = $dd0f
CLK_PERIOD = 985

; Slots in msCur/msMax. MS_GAME is this program's own addition: the door
; logic is the only part of a frame gpu64 cannot account for, so it gets a
; phase of its own and row 10 says what it costs.
MS_PER  = 0
MS_CAM  = 1
MS_WAIT = 2
MS_CMT  = 3
MS_DISP = 4
MS_HB   = 5
MS_GAME = 6
MS_SLOTS = 7

DISP_EVERY = 2

; 26 was right at a 90 degree lens. At 75 the picture is magnified by
; tan(45)/tan(37.5) = 1.30, and the same step read as a much slower walk on
; the bench, so the step is scaled by the same factor. Run 40 still called
; 34 slow at the bench's ~32 frames a second, so the walk is now twice that
; and the run (left SHIFT) twice the walk -- which keeps the run at the 136
; the examples.sh route was flooded with.
WALK  = 68
TURN  = 512

; Motion is per REAL time, not per frame. Every step above (and GRAV,
; DOOR_STEP, PITCH_STEP, the monsters' GR_STEP/DOG_STEP/LEAP_STEP) is what
; one DT_REF-millisecond frame moves, and dtFrame scales it by the measured
; period of the frame before, so a heavy frame covers more ground instead
; of slowing the game down. The constants were all tuned at the bench's
; ~32 frames a second, which is why DT_REF is 32: at that rate the scale is
; exactly 1 and nothing moves differently from before.
;
; DT_MAX bounds it: the run step is a byte, and 136 * 60 / 32 is 255. A
; frame longer than 60ms (a level load, a restart, a pause) moves as one of
; 60ms, so below ~17 frames a second the game does slow down again -- that
; is also the most a single trace should be asked to cover. DT_MIN keeps a
; frame with no measured time (one that did not commit) from standing still.
; Timers -- think cadence, attack cooldowns, door waits -- count game ticks,
; one per DT_REF ms of real time (tickFrame), not frames.
DT_REF = 32
DT_MIN = 8
DT_MAX = 60

; Looking up and down. camPitch is signed, in 1/256 of a turn, positive UP;
; the wire's pitch is the other way round (a positive pitch looks down), so
; sendCamera sends its negation. 48 is 67 degrees, quake3d's limit. The
; step is twice quake3d's because E1M1 renders at a third of its rate.
PITCH_STEP = 4
PITCH_LIM  = 48

; The field of view, horizontal, in whole degrees: 30..110 in fives, + and -
; move along the table. Bench run 38 had 90 fixed and the world seemed to
; collapse towards the edges while turning; the projection assumes square
; pixels and a TV that stretches 384x272 to 16:9 widens them by 1.26, which
; makes a wide lens look wider still. 75 is the default, confirmed on the
; bench.
FOV_DEG0 = 30
FOV_STEP = 5
FOV_N    = 17
FOV_IX0  = ( 75 - FOV_DEG0 ) / FOV_STEP

CIA_PRA = $dc00
CIA_PRB = $dc01
COL_A   = %11111110
COL_B   = %11111101
COL_C   = %11111011
COL_D   = %01111111
COL_E   = %11011111		; PA5: the + and - keys
KA_F7   = $08
KA_F1   = $10
KA_F3   = $20
KA_F5   = $40		; telemetry on / off
KE_PLUS = $01
KE_MINUS = $08
KB_W    = $02
KB_A    = $04
KB_S    = $20
KB_E    = $40
KB_LSH  = $80		; left SHIFT: run
KC_D    = $04
KD_Q    = $40
KD_SPC  = $10
KD_1    = $01		; weapons: 1 axe, 2 shotgun (column 7) ...
KD_2    = $08
KB_3    = $01		; ... 3 super shotgun, 4 nailgun (column 1)
KB_4    = $08
KD_CBM  = $20		; C= fires, as do B, joystick 2's button and the mouse's
KF_B    = $10		; column 3's B; it is also the row joystick 1's fire pulls
COL_F   = %11110111	; PA3: B
COL_G   = %11101111	; PA4 and PA6: only for keys the controls menu
COL_H   = %10111111	; may bind (gpu64_game_controls.inc)
KD_LARR = $02		; <- : the pause menu (gpu64_game_pause.inc)
KF_H    = $20		; H: the health cheat
KG_I    = $02		; I: the weapons cheat

; The mouse: a 1351 in port 1. PA7:6 = 01 connects port 1's pots to the
; SID, which finishes a measurement every 512 cycles; a reading is good only
; two measurements after port 1 was last disconnected. One count of POT X
; turns 64/65536 of a turn; POT Y is halved into camPitch's 1/256 turns.
;
; The pots are sampled by mouseIrq on every IRQ, MOUSE_SUBS a jiffy, not
; once a frame: a 1351 reports its position modulo 64 counts, so a sample
; that moves 32 or more reads as a move the other way, and the shorter
; the interval, the faster a flick fits in it. The movement accumulates
; in accX/accY and mouseLook takes it once a frame.
;
; Nothing may disconnect port 1 within a reading's settling time. While
; sfxIrq is installed the KERNAL's keyboard scan does not run at all (the
; game never reads the KERNAL's keyboard), so the only disconnect is
; readKeys' own column scan, and readKeys waits for the IRQ rather than
; scan inside the settling time before it. If a scan still ends too
; close -- the IRQ was held off -- the next sample is skipped (potSkip);
; that loses nothing, the next delta covers both intervals.
;
; A wrap that still happens is the worst thing the mouse can do: a fast
; turn read as an equally fast turn the other way, then tripled by the
; acceleration. mouseGuard drops a sample that reverses a fast move
; outright and repeats the previous one instead (see there).
POT_X      = $d419
POT_Y      = $d41a
COL_POT1   = %01111111
MOUSE_PROBE = 64		; frames between pot reads with no mouse seen
MOUSE_SENS  = 4			; the controls menu's MOUSE SPEED 5, msYaw's x64
CIA1_TAHI  = $dc05		; timer A, high byte: the IRQ's countdown
MOUSE_SETTLE = 5		; x256 cycles: a deselect this close to the IRQ
MOUSE_RKWAIT = MOUSE_SETTLE + 2	; readKeys waits for the IRQ inside this
MOUSE_REV    = 12		; mouseGuard: |counts| of a suspect reversal
; The mouse is sampled MOUSE_SUBS times a jiffy: sfxInit runs timer A that
; much faster and sfxIrq plays the effects on every MOUSE_SUBS-th IRQ
; only, so they keep 60 Hz. Four samples a jiffy is four times the speed
; the 1351 can be moved at before a sample wraps; readKeys' wait is what
; keeps the period from going much shorter (it must stay well over
; MOUSE_RKWAIT pages).
MOUSE_SUBS   = 4
TIMER_PAL    = $4025		; the KERNAL's own jiffy, PAL ...
TIMER_NTSC   = $4295		; ... and NTSC
; Turning acceleration. A sample (one jiffy) of up to MOUSE_ACC_T counts
; turns 1:1, so slow aiming keeps its 0.35 degree step; every count past
; it turns three. The 1351 still cannot tell more than 31 counts a sample
; from a wrap, so this is what makes a fast turn possible at all: the
; same flick needs a third of the mouse speed.
MOUSE_ACC_T  = 4
; Caps, so a flick too fast to read turns as far as it can, the right
; way, instead of somewhere random. MOUSE_YCAP: accelerated counts a frame
; -- times msYaw's 160 at the top speed it must stay under half a turn, or
; the turn aliases. MOUSE_PCAP: pitch a frame, in 1/256 turn -- one flick
; can no longer pin the view to PITCH_LIM, where turning looks like nothing.
; MOUSE_HOLD: samples mouseGuard holds the previous counts through a
; reversal -- the length of a flick past the 1351's 31 counts a sample.
MOUSE_YCAP   = 200
MOUSE_PCAP   = 6
MOUSE_HOLD   = 32
	.cerror MOUSE_YCAP * 160 >= $8000, "the yaw cap aliases at speed 9"

; RUN/STOP is ignored -- on the bench phantom RUN/STOPs, from ghosting
; and from single bad samples, ended the game with nobody asking. Only
; tools/prgsim/runsim.py can end a run: while its --stop-after schedule
; holds RUN/STOP down it also writes SIM_EXIT_ON here, a byte nothing on a
; real C64 writes after the program clears it.
SIM_EXIT    = $02a7
SIM_EXIT_ON = $a7
; And the same for runsim.py --notarget: the monsters never wake, so a
; route check is not also a fight (gpu64_game_ai.inc).
SIM_NOTARGET    = $02a8
SIM_NOTARGET_ON = $a8
; And runsim.py --warp: a start position (eye, 16.16 x y z, then a 16-bit
; yaw) at SIM_WARP_POS, armed by SIM_WARP_ON. The game restarts there, once,
; so a check can begin beside the monsters it is about (tools/check_ai.py).
SIM_WARP     = $02a9
SIM_WARP_ON  = $a9
SIM_HOP_ON   = $ab			; --hop: the eye moves, nothing restarts
SIM_WARP_POS = $02b0
; And runsim.py --hurt: 20 damage to the player, once per arming -- what
; the <- key did before it became the pause menu (tools/check_combat.py
; dies by it).
SIM_HURT     = $02ad
SIM_HURT_ON  = $ad
SIM_HURT_DMG = 20
; And telemetry: the C64 screen's rows are off by default (they cost ~2000
; cycles a frame), F5 turns them on. runsim.py writes SIM_TELE_ON here
; unless --telemetry-off, because every check reads its verdict off them.
SIM_TELE     = $02aa
SIM_TELE_ON  = $aa
TELE_HOLD    = 4		; frames F5 is held before it toggles

; Frames a key must be held before it acts (see readKeys). The simulator's
; exit waits longest; + and - then repeat every FOV_REPEAT.
STOP_HOLD  = 4
FOV_HOLD   = 3
FOV_REPEAT = 8

CAM_X = 0
CAM_Y = 4
CAM_Z = 8
CAM_BUF_LEN = 18

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; CLIP_MOVE's block. The first 56 bytes are gpu64_demo_level.a's; what is
; new is the mover array that follows it, one 16-byte record per brush model
; this frame wants traced along with the world.
;
; A mover record is a REQUEST, not state: the Pi keeps no list of open doors
; and never has to be told one closed. Every call re-states the whole set,
; which is docs/state-refresh.md's rule applied to collision -- a corrupted
; record costs one frame of one door's hull and nothing more.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
CLIP_IN_LEN  = 32
CLIP_HULL    = 24
CLIP_MODE    = 25
CLIP_MODEL   = 26
CLIP_MAGIC   = 27
CLIP_CHECK   = 28
CLIP_MOVERS  = 29
CLIP_OUT     = 32
CLIP_OFLAGS  = 44
CLIP_OCONT   = 45
CLIP_OFRAC   = 46
CLIP_OBUMPS  = 47
CLIP_OMAGIC  = 48
CLIP_OCHECK  = 49
CLIP_OWL     = 50		; Quake's waterlevel, outside the checksum
CLIP_OWLC    = 51		; and its complement: 0/0 is old firmware
CLIP_MVOFF   = 56
CLIP_MVREC   = 16
CLIP_IN_M    = $c5
CLIP_OUT_M   = $5c

; How many movers one call may carry. The firmware's ceiling is 16
; (GPU64_3D_CLIPMOVE_MAX_MOVERS) and this program uses all of it; the
; selection below is what decides WHICH 16.
SEL_MAX = 16
CLIP_LEN = CLIP_MVOFF + SEL_MAX * CLIP_MVREC

CLIP_MODE_WALK = $01 | $02 | $04 | $08
CLIP_HULL_PLAYER = 1

; How far past the step it asked for an answer is still allowed to land, in
; 1/256ths of a world unit (1 Quake unit = 8). Run 38's bound was two WHOLE
; units -- 64 Quake units, a player's height -- and compared integer halves
; only, so a corrupted answer that got past the checksum could still move the
; player a body length in a frame. That is the "avatar moves on its own" of
; run 39, and one of those moves through a floor is a player outside the map.
;   x, z  a slide never lengthens an axis; 4 Quake units is the epsilon and
;         the truncation with room to spare.
;   y     the trace adds a step-up (18) or a floor settle (18) on its own;
;         37 Quake units covers either on top of the asked-for fall.
POS_SLOP_XZ = $0020
POS_SLOP_Y  = $0128

; How many times to ask again before giving the frame up. The Pi never writes
; the input half, so a retry is the descriptor's six register writes and the
; dispatch -- cheap next to a frame of standing still. Run 40 failed 1.2
; attempts a frame (N 088C M 0917 in 0E41 frames); at that rate four tries
; still gave up about one frame in eight, which reads as a sticky, slow
; walk. Eight gives up about one in sixty.
CLIP_TRIES = 8

FALL = 64
FALL_16 = FALL * 256

; The jump, in the 16.16 world units mov carries (1 Quake unit = $0800).
; Quake's is 270 up against 800 gravity, a 45-unit apex. The first cut was
; 7 Quake units up and 0.54 off each frame after -- 26 frames in the air,
; which at the bench's ~32 frames a second run 41 called "about two times
; longer" than it should be. Twice the speed against four times the
; gravity keeps the 45-unit apex (v^2 / 2g) and halves the time: 14 up,
; 2.16 off, about 13 frames. FALL_16 is both the terminal speed and the
; probe a standing player sends every frame, so a walk-off and a landing
; behave exactly as they did before there was a jump.
JUMP_V  = $7000
GRAV    = $1140
VEL_MIN = $10000 - FALL_16	; -FALL_16 as a 16-bit signed velocity
JUMP_HOLD = 2			; frames SPACE must read down: a phantom
				; keyboard sample is one frame (run 38c)

; How many frames off the ground before the player is declared lost and put
; back where they last stood. The longest real drop in E1M1 is a few hundred
; Quake units, about 40 frames at terminal speed; the void is forever.
VOID_FRAMES = 120

; Climbing out of a mover that shut through the player: CLIMB is 16 Quake
; units a frame (fixed in cmTake), and CLIMB_MAX frames -- 24 world units,
; higher than anything in E1M1 moves -- before starting over from lastGood.
CLIMB_MAX = 48

CLIP_MODE_AIR = CLIP_MODE_WALK & ~($02 | $04)	; no step, no floor settle
CLIP_MODE_SWIM = CLIP_MODE_WALK & ~$04	; no floor settle: water holds you up
CLIP_ONGROUND = $01
CLIP_WALL     = $02
CLIP_CEILING  = $04
CLIP_SOLID    = $10 | $20			; STARTSOLID | ALLSOLID

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; LEVEL_ENT's block: 96 bytes, input in 0..3, the Pi writes 16..95. The
; offsets are absolute within the block, which is why they start at 16 --
; Source/Firmware/gpu64_3d.h tabulates the same numbers.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
ENT_LEN    = 96
ENT_IDX    = 0
ENT_MAGIC  = 2
ENT_CHECK  = 3
ENT_OUT    = 16
ENT_ORIGIN = 16
ENT_MINS   = 28
ENT_MAXS   = 40
ENT_TRAVEL = 52
ENT_YAW    = 64
ENT_SPAWN  = 66
ENT_P0     = 68
ENT_P1     = 70
ENT_TARGET = 72
ENT_TNAME  = 74
ENT_NODEF  = 76
ENT_ECOUNT = 78
ENT_NODEC  = 80
ENT_KIND   = 81
ENT_MODEL  = 82
ENT_OMAGIC = 84
ENT_OCHECK = 85
ENT_IN_M   = $c6
ENT_OUT_M  = $6c

; The kinds this program acts on. gpu64_level.h owns the full table; these
; are the two ranges a door game needs, and they are ranges rather than
; classnames precisely so that a level with a class this program has never
; heard of still lands in the right half.
K_DOOR    = 11			; func_door
K_SECRET  = 12			; func_door_secret
K_PLAT    = 13			; func_plat
K_BUTTON  = 14			; func_button
K_MOVER_1 = 11			; movers are 11..16
K_MOVER_2 = 16
K_TRIG_1  = 21			; trigger volumes are 21..28
K_TRIG_2  = 28
K_TRIG_ONCE   = 21
K_TRIG_MULTI  = 22
K_TRIG_SECRET = 23
K_TRIG_TELE   = 24		; trigger_teleport

; How many of each this program can hold. The shareware episode's worst
; cases are E1M6's 58 movers and E1M4's 45 triggers (E1M1 needs 29 and 26,
; E1M2 32 and 25); anything past these is counted in DROP on row 16 rather
; than silently ignored, because "the level had more doors than the program"
; is a fact a reader of the screen needs and not an error.
MAX_MOV = 60
MAX_TRG = 48
MAX_TEL = 8			; working teleports in one level
FIRE_Q  = 8			; targetnames queued in one frame

; Mover states.
MV_CLOSED  = 0
MV_OPENING = 1
MV_OPEN    = 2
MV_CLOSING = 3

; The animation. frac is 0..255 of the travel, DOOR_STEP per frame, so a
; door takes eight frames to open -- about a second at the frame rate E1M1
; actually renders at. OPEN_WAIT is how many frames it stays open.
DOOR_STEP = 32
OPEN_WAIT = 30

; The touch tests, in FINE units: 1/256 of a world unit, which is 1/8 of a
; Quake unit. Fine units are what this program compares positions in --
; they are the middle two bytes of a 16.16 coordinate, so converting is
; taking two bytes and no arithmetic at all, and the whole of E1M1 fits in
; a signed 16-bit fine value (-19 .. +96 world units).
;
; TOUCH_XZ is Quake's own 60 units of slack around a door (SV_TouchLinks
; sees the door's trigger field, which spawn_door builds from the panel box
; grown by 60 on both horizontal axes); TOUCH_Y is 64 Quake units, which is
; generous on purpose -- the position this program holds is the EYE, and a
; door panel's box is measured from the floor.
TOUCH_XZ = 480			; 60 QU
TOUCH_Y  = 512			; 64 QU
; A trigger volume is the brush itself, so it gets the player's half-width
; horizontally and, vertically, the span from the feet to the top of the
; head measured from the eye.
TRIG_XZ  = 128			; 16 QU
TRIG_YLO = 448			; 56 QU below the eye
TRIG_YHI = 192			; 24 QU above it

; How near a mover has to be to be worth tracing against, in whole world
; units. It is not a gameplay number and must not be tuned like one: one
; frame's step is under a world unit and the player hull is under two, so
; 24 is a factor of ten of margin, and its only job is to keep the far side
; of the map out of the per-frame cost.
;
; The measured effect on E1M1 is the point of it: nine movers within range
; at the worst place on the map, against twenty-nine in the level and a
; sixteen-mover ceiling in CLIP_MOVE. Without it every frame sorts
; twenty-nine boxes into a full list and hands the trace sixteen doors on
; the other side of the building.
NEAR_WU = 24
TOUCH_FAR = 4			; wu: past every expansion (TOUCH_XZ, TRIG_XZ) plus one

FENCE_ECHO = (<healthBuf) ^ (>healthBuf) ^ (<DMA_LEN) ^ (>DMA_LEN) ^ $a5

HB_LEN   = 132
HB_MAGIC = 128
HB_PWR   = 4
HB_TEMP  = 8
HB_DTO   = 12
HB_SLOW  = 16
HB_FLIPS = 18

; One window over every buffer the Pi writes into, so the five must stay
; adjacent and in this order.
DMA_LEN = HB_LEN + CAM_BUF_LEN + CLIP_LEN + ENT_LEN + WT_LEN

HB_EVERY = 64

	#basicStub

	jsr dmInit
	jsr trapInit
	lda #0
	sta SIM_EXIT
	sta SIM_NOTARGET
	sta SIM_WARP
	sta SIM_TELE
	sta SIM_HURT

	; The HONDANI logo, the Quake title and the skill menu
	; (gpu64_game_title.inc). It leaves BASIC banked out for good: the
	; tunes live under it.
	jsr title			; also picks lvSlot, the start level
	jsr sfxInit			; the KERNAL's 60 Hz IRQ is back

; The level change comes back here from the intermission (gpu64_game_exit.inc)
; with lvSlot set to the next level: everything from SCENE_RESET on is per level, and running
; all of it again is what guarantees nothing of E1M1 leaks into E1M2.
levelSetup
	lda #0
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "GPU64 "
	.byte 0
	jsr lvName
	jsr dmText
	.text " - QUAKE ON A C64"
	.byte 0

; ======================================================================
; The view, then the level. Identical to gpu64_demo_level.a: the viewport
; belongs to the caller and has to exist before LOAD_LEVEL, and SCENE_RESET
; has to come before the viewport because it restores the state defaults.
; ======================================================================
	lda #1
	sta CMD_HI

	lda #KEY_DESTRUCTIVE
	sta ARG+15
	lda #OP_SCENE_RESET
	jsr cmd1
	sta resErr

	#argw 0, 0
	#argw 2, 0
	#argw 4, VP_W
	#argw 6, VP_H
	lda #OP_SET_VIEWPORT
	jsr cmd1
	sta vpErr

	lda #FOV_IX0
	sta fovIx
	lda #0
	sta camPitch
	jsr readKeys
	jsr mouseReset
	jsr rgPersp
	sta perErr

	jsr rgBackground

	; The light the level is drawn with. It is the state default, and is
	; sent anyway: a value this program set is a value the refresh ring can
	; put back, where a default nobody wrote has no writer at all.
	jsr rgLight

	lda #2
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "LOAD    "
	.byte 0

loadLevel
	#argw 0, MESH_BASE
	#argw 2, NODE_BASE
	#argw 4, CAM_ID
	; ARG6 is the level slot: slot n-1 of the REU level pack for E1Mn.
	; ARG7 = ARG6 ^ $5A or the Pi takes slot 0.
	lda lvSlot
	sta ARG+6
	eor #$5a
	sta ARG+7
	lda #KEY_DESTRUCTIVE
	sta ARG+15
	lda #OP_LOAD_LEVEL
	jsr cmd1
	sta loadErr
	beq loadArmed
	; A slot the Pi has no level for answers BAD_ARGS (a smaller pack in
	; the REU): play E1M1 rather than end the game.
	ldx lvSlot
	beq +
	cmp #ERR_BAD_ARGS
	bne +
	lda #0
	sta lvSlot
	lda #0
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "GPU64 E1M1 - THAT LEVEL IS NOT LOADED "
	.byte 0
	jmp loadLevel
+	jsr showLoadErr
	jmp finish

loadArmed
	lda #0
	sta stepCt
	sta stepCt+1

stepLoop
	; Checked, with no arguments: a LEVEL_STEP that arrives with any byte
	; flipped -- CMD_HI into a class that does not exist was the one that
	; ended runsim --bus-fault=data:97 at step $1D -- is refused before it
	; runs and sent again by stage2, where it used to end the program.
	lda #0
	sta stId
	sta stId+1
	lda #OP_LEVEL_STEP
	ldx #$ff
	jsr stage2
	sta stepErr
	bne stepFailed

	inc stepCt
	bne +
	inc stepCt+1
+
	; Believed only when two reads agree. A read the loop misses returns the
	; last bus value, very often $FF -- which is LEVEL_DONE, and runsim
	; --bus-fault=both:97 duly ended the load after two steps of 29.
	ldx #8
-	lda RESULT
	cmp RESULT
	beq +
	dex
	bne -
+	sta stepPct
	cmp #LEVEL_DONE
	beq stepDone

	lda #2
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "LOAD    "
	.byte 0
	lda stepPct
	jsr dmHexA
	jmp stepLoop

stepFailed
	jsr showLoadErr
	jmp finish

stepDone
	lda #2
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "LOAD    DONE IN "
	.byte 0
	lda stepCt+1
	jsr dmHexA
	lda stepCt
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " STEPS"
	.byte 0

	#id16 CAM_ID
	lda #OP_SET_ACTIVE_CAMERA
	jsr cmd1
	sta camErr

	jsr seedCamera

	; The gun and the status bar take the first actor slots, so entScan's
	; items and monsters are appended after them.
	jsr actInit

	; The entity lump, one record at a time. This is the part that makes
	; the program a game rather than a walk, and it happens exactly once:
	; after it, nothing in the frame loop reads the level file again.
	jsr entScan
	jsr pjInit			; the missiles' slots, after the level's
	jsr combatInit
	jsr combatCount
	jsr aiInit

	; And every level node's position, read back once so the ring below
	; has something to put back -- see nodeScan.
	jsr nodeMark

	; Every actor's node, then three blocks to pose them all (a block
	; carries thirty dirty records; the frame loop would get there too).
	jsr actCreate
	jsr worldTick
	jsr worldTick
	jsr worldTick
	jsr mfInit			; the muzzle flash's light node

	lda #0
	sta ARG+0
	lda #OP_LOOP_START
	jsr cmd1
	sta loopErr

	lda #3
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "SETUP   "
	.byte 0
	lda vpErr
	ora perErr
	ora resErr
	ora camErr
	ora getErr
	beq setupOk
	jsr dmText
	.text "VP "
	.byte 0
	lda vpErr
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " PER "
	.byte 0
	lda perErr
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " RST "
	.byte 0
	lda resErr
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " CAM "
	.byte 0
	lda camErr
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " GET "
	.byte 0
	lda getErr
	jsr dmHexA
	jmp setupShown
setupOk
	jsr dmText
	.text "OK  LOOP "
	.byte 0
	lda loopErr
	jsr dmHexA
setupShown

	lda #0
	sta frames
	sta frames+1
	sta okFrames
	sta okFrames+1
	sta e06
	sta e06+1
	sta e07
	sta e07+1
	sta eXX
	sta eXX+1
	sta xxCode
	sta missed
	sta missed+1
	sta stalls
	sta dispCt
	sta dispCt+1
	sta dispCt+2
	sta stalls+1
	ldx #MS_SLOTS-1
-	sta msCur,x
	sta msMax,x
	dex
	bpl -
	lda #$ff
	sta msPerMin
	lda #1
	sta dispWait
	sta hbWait
	lda #0
	sta hbBad

	jsr exReset
	jsr clkInit
	jsr teleShown

; ======================================================================
; A frame.
;
; The order is not arbitrary. Selection reads the camera BEFORE the move,
; because the movers whose hulls this frame is traced against have to be in
; the block the move is asked with. The touch tests run AFTER it, from where
; the player actually ended up -- standing in a doorway is a fact about the
; new position, not the old one. Animation is last, so the displacement a
; door's hull is traced with next frame is the one its node is drawn at this
; frame, and the two can never disagree by more than the frame they were
; sent in.
; ======================================================================
frame
	jsr clkPace
	jsr clkFrame
	jsr exFrame			; the play time, the message row
	jsr simWarp
	jsr moveCamera			; keys -> mov, the requested step
	jsr gameSelect			; the nearest movers, for the trace
	jsr clipMove			; the step, collided; updates cam
	jsr gameTouch			; what the new position opens
	jsr combatTouch			; what it picks up
	jsr envFrame			; what it is standing in
	lda tick			; the rest of the logic runs on ticks
	beq frNoTick1			; (tickFrame)
	jsr tickLogic
	jsr gameAnimate			; state machines, and the new ofs
frNoTick1
	ldx #MS_GAME
	jsr clkLap
	jsr sendMovers			; SET_POSITION for what moved
	lda tick
	beq frNoTick2
	jsr combatFrame			; weapons, a shot's traces, the HUD
	jsr aiFrame			; monsters think; their traces and moves
	jsr actFrame			; animate, repair one, WORLD_TICK
	jsr combatResolve		; the shot, from the traces' answers
	jsr aiResolve			; the monsters', from theirs
frNoTick2
	jsr sendCamera
	jsr sendView			; SET_PERSPECTIVE, if + or - moved it
	jsr doRefresh			; one piece of retained state, re-sent
	jsr mfFrame			; the muzzle flash
	ldx #MS_CAM
	jsr clkLap
	jsr waitReady
	ldx #MS_WAIT
	jsr clkLap

	; Checked like everything stage2 sends; with no ID and no arguments the
	; check is a constant. A commit whose CMD_LO arrives as some other
	; opcode is then refused instead of obeyed, and the next frame commits.
	lda #0
	sta ID_LO
	sta ID_HI
	lda #KEY_CHECKED ^ 1 ^ OP_SCENE_COMMIT
	sta ARG+14
	lda #KEY_CHECKED
	sta ARG+15
	lda #OP_SCENE_COMMIT
	jsr cmdSeq1
	ldx #MS_CMT
	jsr clkLap
	lda seqErr
	beq frOk

	cmp #ERR_BUSY
	bne frNot07
	inc e07
	bne frCount
	inc e07+1
	jmp frCount
frNot07
	cmp #ERR_UNSUPPORTED
	bne frNotXX
	inc e06
	bne frCount
	inc e06+1
	jmp frCount
frNotXX
	sta xxCode
	inc eXX
	bne frCount
	inc eXX+1
	jmp frCount
frOk
	inc okFrames
	bne frCount
	inc okFrames+1
frCount
	inc frames
	bne +
	inc frames+1
+
	dec hbWait
	bne frNoHealth
	jsr doHealth
	ldx #MS_HB
	jsr clkLap
frNoHealth

	lda SIM_TELE
	cmp #SIM_TELE_ON
	bne +
	sta teleOn			; (non-zero)
+	lda teleOn
	beq frNoShow
	jsr showSlice
	ldx #MS_DISP
	jsr clkLap
frNoShow

	lda exitHit
	beq +
	jsr intermission		; the exit: stats, then the level again
+	jsr pauseCheck			; <-: the pause menu
	lda SIM_EXIT
	cmp #SIM_EXIT_ON
	beq frStopKey
	lda #0
	sta stopHold
	jmp frame
frStopKey
	inc stopHold
	lda stopHold
	cmp #STOP_HOLD
	bcs frDone
	jmp frame
frDone
	jmp finish

; ======================================================================
; Shutting down. LOOP_STOP is what hands the framebuffer back.
; ======================================================================
finish
	jsr sfxOff
	lda #1
	sta CMD_HI
	ldx #8
fnStop
	lda #KEY_DESTRUCTIVE
	sta ARG+15
	lda #OP_LOOP_STOP
	jsr cmdSeq1
	lda seqMiss
	beq fnStopped
	dex
	bne fnStop
fnStopped
	stx stopTry

	lda #0
	sta CMD_HI

	jsr showFrame

	lda #1
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "STOPPED"
	.byte 0

	; The simulator's release, then its second press: runsim counts the
	; column-7 reads readKeys makes, so the schedule advances here too.
	; Not after the intermission: its matrix scans have run the whole
	; schedule through already, and the key reads down for good.
	lda fnQuick
	bne fnOut
-	jsr readKeys
	lda SIM_EXIT
	cmp #SIM_EXIT_ON
	beq -
-	jsr readKeys
	lda SIM_EXIT
	cmp #SIM_EXIT_ON
	bne -
fnOut
	jsr $e544
	lda #$37			; BASIC back: title banked it out
	sta $01
	rts

; ======================================================================
; The camera. THE C64 OWNS IT -- see gpu64_demo_level.a for why every send
; is absolute and never a delta. Nothing here is different in this program;
; what is different is that the world it walks through now moves.
; ======================================================================

seedCamera
	jsr setDmaWin

	ldx #4
scGet
	stx getTry

	lda #$a5
	ldy #CAM_BUF_LEN-1
-	sta camBuf,y
	dey
	bpl -

	#id16 CAM_ID
	#argblob 0, SPACE_C64, camBuf, CAM_BUF_LEN
	lda #OP_GET_TRANSFORM
	jsr cmd1
	sta getErr
	bne scRetry

	lda camBuf+14
	ora camBuf+15
	ora camBuf+16
	ora camBuf+17
	beq scGot
	lda #$ff
	sta getErr
scRetry
	ldx getTry
	dex
	bne scGet
	rts

scGot
	ldx #11
-	lda camBuf,x
	sta cam,x
	sta lastGood,x
	dex
	bpl -
	lda camBuf+12
	sta camYaw
	lda camBuf+13
	sta camYaw+1
	rts

; The readback fence, with its echo checked and three tries. Also a refresh
; ring entry: a phantom SET_DMA_WINDOW would aim the next health block at
; this program's code, and nothing else would ever move the window back.
; Returns with CMD_HI = 0; every caller's next command is a cmd1, which sets
; it again.
setDmaWin
	ldx #3
scFence
	stx fenceTry
	#argw 0, healthBuf
	#argw 2, DMA_LEN
	lda #OP_SET_DMA_WINDOW
	jsr cmdClass0
	sta fenceErr
	lda RESULT
	sta fenceEcho
	cmp #FENCE_ECHO
	beq scFenced
	ldx fenceTry
	dex
	bne scFence
scFenced
	rts

moveCamera
	jsr readKeys
	jsr deadKeys
	jsr viewKeys
	jsr mouseLook

	lda #0
	ldx #11
-	sta mov,x
	dex
	bpl -

	lda walkStep
	sta stepLo
	lda keyB
	and #KB_LSH
	bne mcStepSet
	lda runStep
	sta stepLo
mcStepSet
	jsr swimCheck			; waist deep: a slower step, no gravity

	; SPACE jumps, once per press and only from the ground. It must read
	; down JUMP_HOLD frames in a row first: column 7 is sampled once a
	; frame, and a single phantom sample is exactly what ended run 38.
	lda keyD
	and #KD_SPC
	beq mcSpcDown
	lda #0
	sta spcHold
	beq mcJumpDone
mcSpcDown
	lda swimming
	bne mcJumpDone			; in the water SPACE swims up
	lda spcHold
	cmp #JUMP_HOLD
	bcs mcJumpDone			; held: one jump per press
	inc spcHold
	lda spcHold
	cmp #JUMP_HOLD
	bne mcJumpDone
	lda onGround
	beq mcJumpDone
	lda #<JUMP_V
	sta velY
	lda #>JUMP_V
	sta velY+1
	lda #0
	sta onGround
	lda #1
	sta jumping
	lda #SFX_JUMP
	jsr sfxPlay
	inc jumps
mcJumpDone

	lda keyB
	and #KB_A
	bne mcNotLeft
	sec
	lda camYaw
	sbc turnD
	sta camYaw
	lda camYaw+1
	sbc turnD+1
	sta camYaw+1
mcNotLeft
	lda keyC
	and #KC_D
	bne mcNotRight
	clc
	lda camYaw
	adc turnD
	sta camYaw
	lda camYaw+1
	adc turnD+1
	sta camYaw+1
mcNotRight

	lda keyB
	and #KB_W
	bne mcNotFwd
	lda #0
	sta dirNeg
	jsr mcWalk
mcNotFwd
	lda keyB
	and #KB_S
	bne mcNotBack
	lda #1
	sta dirNeg
	jsr mcWalk
mcNotBack
	lda keyD
	and #KD_Q
	bne mcNotStrafeL
	lda #1
	sta dirNeg
	jsr mcStrafe
mcNotStrafeL
	lda keyB
	and #KB_E
	bne mcNotStrafeR
	lda #0
	sta dirNeg
	jsr mcStrafe
mcNotStrafeR

	; The vertical step is the velocity, scaled by the frame time and
	; sign-extended into the 16.16 delta. Standing, velY is VEL_MIN -- the
	; same FALL_16 probe every frame that there always was, which is what
	; makes the floor settle find the floor -- so nothing about walking
	; changed when the jump arrived.
	lda velY
	sta mA
	lda velY+1
	sta mA+1
	jsr dtMulS
	; Added, not stored: a swimmer's walk has already put the pitch's
	; share of the step into mov's y.
	ldx #0
	lda dtR+2
	bpl +
	dex
+	clc
	lda dtR
	adc mov+CAM_Y+0
	sta mov+CAM_Y+0
	lda dtR+1
	adc mov+CAM_Y+1
	sta mov+CAM_Y+1
	lda dtR+2
	adc mov+CAM_Y+2
	sta mov+CAM_Y+2
	txa
	adc mov+CAM_Y+3
	sta mov+CAM_Y+3

	lda swimming
	beq +
	rts				; swimCheck has set velY already
+

	; Gravity, down to terminal speed. Signed 16-bit: subtract, then clamp
	; anything below VEL_MIN (or wrapped past it) back to VEL_MIN.
	sec
	lda velY
	sbc gravD
	sta velY
	lda velY+1
	sbc gravD+1
	sta velY+1
	bvs mcClamp			; wrapped past -32768
	bpl mcGravDone			; still rising, or zero-ish
	cmp #>VEL_MIN
	bcc mcClamp
	bne mcGravDone
	lda velY
	cmp #<VEL_MIN
	bcs mcGravDone
mcClamp
	lda #<VEL_MIN
	sta velY
	lda #>VEL_MIN
	sta velY+1
mcGravDone
	rts

; ======================================================================
; CLIP_MOVE, with movers.
;
; The block is the level demo's, plus selCnt 16-byte records at CLIP_MVOFF
; and a checksum that now covers them: the count byte, bytes 0..27, and
; every mover byte. A door whose record is lost is a door you walk through
; -- which is exactly why the checksum was widened rather than left over
; the fixed half.
;
; A rejected answer is retried up to CLIP_TRIES times and only then allowed
; to do nothing. The Pi never writes the input half, so a retry is the six
; register writes of the descriptor plus the dispatch; run 37 rejected 61% of
; this program's answers, three times the level demo's rate, which is a lot
; of frames to stand still for when the retry is that cheap.
; ======================================================================
clipMove
	ldx #11
-	lda cam,x
	sta clipBlk,x
	lda mov,x
	sta clipBlk+12,x
	dex
	bpl -

	lda #CLIP_HULL_PLAYER
	sta clipBlk+CLIP_HULL
	; A jump flies until it lands: no step-up (which would climb a ledge
	; the jump did not reach) and no floor settle (which traces down 18
	; units and would snap a rising player straight back onto the floor).
	lda #CLIP_MODE_WALK
	ldx jumping
	beq +
	lda #CLIP_MODE_AIR
+	ldx swimming
	beq +
	lda #CLIP_MODE_SWIM
+	sta clipBlk+CLIP_MODE
	lda #0
	sta clipBlk+CLIP_MODEL		; the world, plus the movers below
	sta clipBlk+CLIP_MVOFF-2	; the two reserved bytes, which the
	sta clipBlk+CLIP_MVOFF-1	; Pi neither reads nor sums
	lda #CLIP_IN_M
	sta clipBlk+CLIP_MAGIC

	jsr buildMovers

	lda clipBlk+CLIP_MOVERS
	ldx #0
-	eor clipBlk,x
	inx
	cpx #CLIP_CHECK
	bne -
	eor bmChk			; the mover bytes: buildMovers XORed
	sta clipChk			; them as it copied (the zeroes add
	sta clipBlk+CLIP_CHECK		; nothing)

	ldx #CLIP_TRIES
	stx cmTry
cmSend
	lda #$a5
	ldx #CLIP_OUT
-	sta clipBlk,x
	inx
	cpx #CLIP_MVOFF
	bne -

	#argblob 0, SPACE_C64, clipBlk, CLIP_LEN
	lda #OP_CLIP_MOVE
	jsr cmd1
	sta clipErr

	; ERRCODE is a diagnostic here and never a verdict. It is one register
	; read, and a register read that fails hands back whatever was last on
	; the bus rather than an error (gpu64-errcode-ff-is-the-bus), so
	; treating a garbled read as a refusal throws away good answers --
	; which is how run 27 died. When the command really WAS refused the Pi
	; wrote nothing, the poison is still in place, and the magic test below
	; catches it anyway. docs/class1-3d-mesh-reference.md says exactly
	; this: check the block, not ERRCODE.
	cmp #0
	beq cmErrOK
	inc clipN
	bne cmErrOK
	inc clipN+1
cmErrOK
	lda clipBlk+CLIP_OMAGIC
	cmp #CLIP_OUT_M
	beq cmMagOK
	inc clipM
	bne cmAgain
	inc clipM+1
	jmp cmAgain
cmMagOK
	lda #0
	ldx #CLIP_OUT
-	eor clipBlk,x
	inx
	cpx #CLIP_OCHECK
	bne -
	cmp clipBlk+CLIP_OCHECK
	beq cmSumOK
	inc clipS
	bne cmAgain
	inc clipS+1
	jmp cmAgain
cmSumOK

	lda clipBlk+CLIP_OFLAGS
	sta clipFlags
	lda clipBlk+CLIP_OCONT
	sta clipCont
	lda clipBlk+CLIP_OFRAC
	sta clipFrac
	lda clipBlk+CLIP_OBUMPS
	sta clipBumps
	; The water level rides outside the checksum (it came later), so it
	; carries its own complement; a pair that fails keeps last frame's.
	lda clipBlk+CLIP_OWL
	cmp #4
	bcs +
	eor #$ff
	cmp clipBlk+CLIP_OWLC
	bne +
	eor #$ff
	jsr setWater
+	jmp cmTake

	; The Pi never touches the input half, so another try is the six
	; register writes of the descriptor plus the dispatch -- far cheaper
	; than a frame of standing still, and at run 37's fault rate the
	; second try is usually the one that lands. Only the poison has to be
	; laid down again, which is why cmSend is where it is.
cmAgain
	dec cmTry
	beq cmDry
	jmp cmSend
cmWild
	lda clipWild
	cmp #$ff
	beq cmDry
	inc clipWild
	; A frame with no answer taken is a frame the player could not move.
	; DRY on row 19 is how many of those in a row, and its worst: run 40
	; lost movement for good after a minute, and a photo of two running
	; totals could not say whether CLIP_MOVE had stopped answering or the
	; player was standing somewhere nothing could leave.
cmDry
	inc clipDry
	bne +
	dec clipDry			; saturates at 255
+	lda clipDry
	cmp clipDryMax
	bcc +
	sta clipDryMax
+	rts

cmTake
	; Inside solid is not a move to refuse, it is a place to leave. Run 38
	; skipped a STARTSOLID answer and took an ALLSOLID one, and either way
	; the eye stayed where it was -- forever, because the next question
	; starts from the same place. Quake's SV_CheckStuck puts the player
	; back where they last stood, and so does this.
	;
	; Once is a bad answer and lastGood fixes it. Again straight after is
	; a place that has itself become solid, and the route found the case:
	; door 29 is a lift that lowers, the player rides it down, and when it
	; shuts it rises straight through them. Quake's mover would carry the
	; player up; the Pi's does not push, so the client climbs: every
	; further stuck frame puts the eye CLIMB higher above lastGood, until
	; an answer comes back clear and the floor settle stands them on top.
	lda clipFlags
	and #CLIP_SOLID
	beq cmNotSolid
	inc clipStuck
	bne +
	dec clipStuck			; saturate at $ff
+	lda stuckRun
	cmp #CLIMB_MAX
	bcc +
	lda #0				; nothing above either: start over
	sta stuckRun
+	inc stuckRun
	jsr restoreGood
	; cam.y += (stuckRun - 1) * CLIMB, CLIMB = $80 in byte 1
	ldx stuckRun
	dex
	txa
	lsr a
	sta cbD1			; high: n >> 1
	lda #0
	ror a				; low: (n & 1) << 7
	clc
	adc cam+CAM_Y+1
	sta cam+CAM_Y+1
	lda cbD1
	adc cam+CAM_Y+2
	sta cam+CAM_Y+2
	lda #0
	adc cam+CAM_Y+3
	sta cam+CAM_Y+3
	rts
cmNotSolid
	lda #0
	sta stuckRun

	jsr cmBound
	bcs cmWild
	lda #0
	sta clipDry

	; The answer is taken, so the question's start was not in solid: if
	; the player was standing there, it is a place to come back to.
	lda onGround
	beq cmNoSave
	ldx #11
-	lda cam,x
	sta lastGood,x
	dex
	bpl -
cmNoSave

	ldx #11
-	lda clipBlk+CLIP_OUT,x
	sta cam,x
	dex
	bpl -

	; Landing. ONGROUND only counts on the way down: a rising jump that
	; grazes a step edge is still a jump.
	lda clipFlags
	and #CLIP_ONGROUND
	beq cmAir
	lda velY+1
	bpl cmAir
	lda swimming
	bne cmSwimFloor
	lda #1
	sta onGround
	lda #0
	sta jumping
	sta airFrames
	lda #<VEL_MIN
	sta velY
	lda #>VEL_MIN
	sta velY+1
	rts
	; A swimmer on the bottom just stops sinking. The walker's landing
	; would set the fall probe, and swimCheck's average would never climb
	; back out of it: SPACE could not lift a swimmer off the floor.
cmSwimFloor
	lda #0
	sta velY
	sta velY+1
	sta airFrames
	rts
cmAir
	lda #0
	sta onGround
	; A head hit ends the rise; the fall that follows is ordinary.
	lda clipFlags
	and #CLIP_CEILING
	beq +
	lda velY+1
	bmi +
	lda #0
	sta velY
	sta velY+1
+
	; Swimming is not falling: the void count is for a player with nothing
	; under them, and a swimmer may never touch the bottom at all.
	lda swimming
	beq +
	lda #0
	sta airFrames
	rts
+
	; The void. Outside the map's hull nothing is solid and nothing is a
	; floor, so the player falls at terminal speed for the rest of the
	; session -- run 39's black HDMI screen with the C64 still counting,
	; at Y -205. No real drop in E1M1 takes VOID_FRAMES. Counted in
	; ticks, so the limit is the same time at any frame rate.
	lda tick
	beq cmDone
	inc airFrames
	lda airFrames
	cmp #VOID_FRAMES
	bcc cmDone
	inc clipVoid
	bne restoreGood
	dec clipVoid
	jmp restoreGood
cmDone
	rts

; Back to the last place the player stood, standing still. Also what a
; bad start does to anything in flight.
restoreGood
	ldx #11
-	lda lastGood,x
	sta cam,x
	dex
	bpl -
	lda #<VEL_MIN
	sta velY
	lda #>VEL_MIN
	sta velY+1
	lda #0
	sta jumping
	sta airFrames
	sta onGround
	rts


; ======================================================================
; Bound the answer by the question.
;
; An answer that passes its checksum and is still wrong is not a one-frame
; glitch. cam is authoritative, nothing in this program ever contradicts it,
; and a corrupted coordinate is therefore the rest of the session. That is
; what run 36 looked like: the eye 222 world units outside E1M1, y and z
; still exactly at the spawn, x $000F arriving as $FF0F -- one byte -- and
; the picture a level seen from the outside, with the player never having
; touched a key.
;
; An 8-bit XOR over seventeen bytes cannot prevent that on its own. It
; passes about one corrupted block in 256, and that run had the checksum
; reject 67 blocks in 345 frames, so one silent acceptance in a session is
; the expected outcome rather than bad luck. A wider checksum would only
; move the exponent.
;
; So this is a different KIND of check, not a stronger hash: a move cannot
; end further from where it started than the step that was asked for, plus
; the step-up the trace is allowed to add on its own. The question bounds
; the answer, and the bound is exact rather than probabilistic.
;
; Run 38's version compared whole units with two of slop, which let a bad
; answer move the player 64 Quake units a frame and still be taken -- run
; 39's player drifting on its own, and at least once through a floor. This
; one works in 1/256ths of a unit (bytes 1..3 of the 16.16 values, so the
; lowest byte is the only thing ignored) with POS_SLOP_XZ / POS_SLOP_Y.
;
; Carry clear = plausible, and cam may take it.
; ======================================================================
cmBound
	ldx #0
cbAxis
	; d = |end - start| over bytes 1..3; anything that does not fit in
	; 16 bits of 1/256ths (256 units) fails outright
	sec
	lda clipBlk+CLIP_OUT+1,x
	sbc cam+1,x
	sta cbD0
	lda clipBlk+CLIP_OUT+2,x
	sbc cam+2,x
	sta cbD1
	lda clipBlk+CLIP_OUT+3,x
	sbc cam+3,x
	bpl cbDPos
	; negate the 24-bit value; its top byte must become 0
	eor #$ff
	sta cbD2
	lda cbD0
	eor #$ff
	clc
	adc #1
	sta cbD0
	lda cbD1
	eor #$ff
	adc #0
	sta cbD1
	lda cbD2
	adc #0
cbDPos
	bne cbFail

	; m = |delta| + slop, the same bytes. A step is well under a unit, so
	; the delta's top byte is sign only.
	lda mov+1,x
	sta cbM0
	lda mov+2,x
	sta cbM1
	lda mov+3,x
	bpl cbMPos
	sec
	lda #0
	sbc cbM0
	sta cbM0
	lda #0
	sbc cbM1
	sta cbM1
cbMPos
	clc
	lda cbM0
	adc cbSlopLo,x
	sta cbM0
	lda cbM1
	adc cbSlopHi,x
	sta cbM1

	; Reject when m < d.
	lda cbM0
	cmp cbD0
	lda cbM1
	sbc cbD1
	bcc cbFail

	inx
	inx
	inx
	inx
	cpx #12
	beq +
	jmp cbAxis
+	clc
	rts
cbFail
	sec
	rts

; Indexed by the axis offset 0/4/8: x, y, z.
cbSlopLo	.byte <POS_SLOP_XZ, 0, 0, 0, <POS_SLOP_Y, 0, 0, 0, <POS_SLOP_XZ
cbSlopHi	.byte >POS_SLOP_XZ, 0, 0, 0, >POS_SLOP_Y, 0, 0, 0, >POS_SLOP_XZ

; The mover records themselves. X walks the block byte by byte and Y the
; mover table, so the record layout below reads in the order
; docs/class1-3d-mesh-reference.md tabulates it.
;
; Only when they changed: gameSelect chose again or a mover moved
; (mulOfs). The Pi writes no further into the block than CLIP_MVOFF, and
; gameSelect's every-other-frame rebuild bounds what a stray write could
; spoil to one frame, which the block's check refuses anyway.
buildMovers
	lda bmStale
	bne +
	rts
+	lda #0
	sta bmStale
	lda selCnt
	sta clipBlk+CLIP_MOVERS
	lda #0
	sta bmChk			; the XOR of every byte copied below
	ldx #0				; byte offset into the array
	ldy #0				; which selected mover
bmLoop
	cpy selCnt
	bne +
	rts
+
	sty bldIx
	lda selIdx,y
	tay				; Y is now the mover table index

	lda mvModel,y
	sta clipBlk+CLIP_MVOFF,x
	eor bmChk
	sta bmChk
	inx
	lda #0
	sta clipBlk+CLIP_MVOFF,x	; flags, reserved
	inx
	sta clipBlk+CLIP_MVOFF,x
	inx
	sta clipBlk+CLIP_MVOFF,x
	inx

	lda mvOX0,y
	sta clipBlk+CLIP_MVOFF,x
	eor bmChk
	sta bmChk
	inx
	lda mvOX1,y
	sta clipBlk+CLIP_MVOFF,x
	eor bmChk
	sta bmChk
	inx
	lda mvOX2,y
	sta clipBlk+CLIP_MVOFF,x
	eor bmChk
	sta bmChk
	inx
	lda mvOX3,y
	sta clipBlk+CLIP_MVOFF,x
	eor bmChk
	sta bmChk
	inx

	lda mvOY0,y
	sta clipBlk+CLIP_MVOFF,x
	eor bmChk
	sta bmChk
	inx
	lda mvOY1,y
	sta clipBlk+CLIP_MVOFF,x
	eor bmChk
	sta bmChk
	inx
	lda mvOY2,y
	sta clipBlk+CLIP_MVOFF,x
	eor bmChk
	sta bmChk
	inx
	lda mvOY3,y
	sta clipBlk+CLIP_MVOFF,x
	eor bmChk
	sta bmChk
	inx

	lda mvOZ0,y
	sta clipBlk+CLIP_MVOFF,x
	eor bmChk
	sta bmChk
	inx
	lda mvOZ1,y
	sta clipBlk+CLIP_MVOFF,x
	eor bmChk
	sta bmChk
	inx
	lda mvOZ2,y
	sta clipBlk+CLIP_MVOFF,x
	eor bmChk
	sta bmChk
	inx
	lda mvOZ3,y
	sta clipBlk+CLIP_MVOFF,x
	eor bmChk
	sta bmChk
	inx

	ldy bldIx
	iny
	jmp bmLoop

; Bench run 38b: one bad sample of a key ended the game with nobody
; touching the keyboard -- the frozen HDMI "hang" -- and bad samples of
; MINUS narrowed the FOV twice. So each column is read twice and a key
; (a 0 bit) is down only if BOTH reads say so; the keys whose effect
; lasts are also held for several frames first (viewKeys, combatFrame).
;
; Both control ports share the matrix. Port 1 (the mouse) drives the ROW
; lines, so whatever it pulls low reads as held in every column: those
; rows are masked out of every column below. Port 2 (the joystick) drives
; the COLUMN lines, so a direction held with a key in its column ghosts
; that key into every scan: while the stick is off centre the keyboard is
; ignored and the stick alone steers.
readKeys
	; Not within MOUSE_RKWAIT pages of a mouse sample: wait the IRQ out
	; instead, so the scan below cannot make it skip. Timer A reloads at
	; the IRQ either way, so this ends even with interrupts off.
-	lda CIA1_TAHI
	cmp #MOUSE_RKWAIT
	bcc -
	sei
	lda #$ff
	sta $dc02
	lda #$00
	sta $dc03

	; Timer A before the scan, for the guard at the end.
	lda CIA1_TAHI
	sta rkTimer

	; Is there a mouse? Only every MOUSE_PROBE frames until there is: with
	; nothing in port 1 both pots read $ff, and a 1351 never reads that.
	; After that mouseIrq does all the reading, and
	; potSync makes its first reading the reference, so being found does
	; not turn the view.
	lda mouseSeen
	bne rkNoPots
	dec mouseProbe
	bpl rkNoPots
	lda #MOUSE_PROBE - 1
	sta mouseProbe
	lda POT_X
	and POT_Y
	cmp #$ff
	beq rkNoPots
	inc mouseSeen
	lda #1
	sta potSync
rkNoPots

	; Port 1 with no column selected.
	lda #$ff
	sta CIA_PRA
	lda CIA_PRB
	sta port1
	eor #$ff
	sta p1Mask

	lda #COL_B
	sta CIA_PRA
	lda CIA_PRB
	ora CIA_PRB
	ora p1Mask
	sta keyB
	lda #COL_C
	sta CIA_PRA
	lda CIA_PRB
	ora CIA_PRB
	ora p1Mask
	sta keyC
	lda #COL_D
	sta CIA_PRA
	lda CIA_PRB		; once: one column-7 read a frame is what
	ora p1Mask		; runsim's --stop-after and check_game.py
	sta keyD		; count by
	lda #COL_A
	sta CIA_PRA
	lda CIA_PRB
	ora CIA_PRB
	ora p1Mask
	sta keyA
	lda #COL_E
	sta CIA_PRA
	lda CIA_PRB
	ora CIA_PRB
	ora p1Mask
	sta keyE
	lda #COL_F
	sta CIA_PRA
	lda CIA_PRB
	ora CIA_PRB
	ora p1Mask
	sta keyF
	lda #COL_G
	sta CIA_PRA
	lda CIA_PRB
	ora CIA_PRB
	ora p1Mask
	sta keyG
	lda #COL_H
	sta CIA_PRA
	lda CIA_PRB
	ora CIA_PRB
	ora p1Mask
	sta keyH

	lda #$ff
	sta CIA_PRA
	lda CIA_PRA		; joystick 2, with no column selected
	sta joy2
	lda #COL_POT1		; port 1's pots measure until the next frame
	sta CIA_PRA
	; The scan disconnected port 1 (COL_H). If the next IRQ's sample would
	; fall inside the settling time, or that IRQ is already pending -- the
	; timer reloaded during the scan, so it reads higher than before --
	; it skips.
	lda CIA1_TAHI
	cmp #MOUSE_SETTLE
	bcc rkSkip
	lda rkTimer
	cmp CIA1_TAHI
	bcs rkCli
rkSkip
	lda #1
	sta potSkip
rkCli
	cli

	; The controls menu's bindings, onto the default keys' bits -- so
	; everything below and after reads W/S/A/D/Q/E/SHIFT/SPACE as before.
	; Before the stick, which replaces the keyboard with those same bits.
	jsr kbRemap

	; The stick: off centre, it replaces the keyboard.
	lda joy2
	and #$0f
	cmp #$0f
	beq rkNoStick
	lda #$ff
	sta keyA
	sta keyB
	sta keyC
	sta keyE
	sta keyF
	sta keyD
	lda joy2
	lsr a
	bcs +
	lda keyB
	and #$ff ^ KB_W
	sta keyB
+	lda joy2
	and #$02
	bne +
	lda keyB
	and #$ff ^ KB_S
	sta keyB
+	lda joy2
	and #$04
	bne +
	lda keyB
	and #$ff ^ KB_A
	sta keyB
+	lda joy2
	and #$08
	bne rkNoStick
	lda keyC
	and #$ff ^ KC_D
	sta keyC
rkNoStick

	; Fire: the mouse's button, the stick's, B or C=. A key in a matrix
	; can be a ghost -- three held keys on three corners of a rectangle
	; make the fourth read as held -- so B and C= count only if they are
	; alone in their column or alone in their row.
	lda #0
	sta fireDown
	lda port1
	and #$10
	bne +
	inc fireDown
+	lda joy2
	and #$10
	bne +
	inc fireDown
+
	lda keyF
	and #KF_B
	bne rkNoB
	lda keyF
	ora #KF_B
	cmp #$ff
	beq rkB
	lda keyA
	and keyB
	and keyC
	and keyD
	and keyE
	and #KF_B
	beq rkNoB
rkB	inc fireDown
rkNoB
	lda keyD
	and #KD_CBM
	bne rkNoCbm
	lda keyD
	ora #KD_CBM
	cmp #$ff
	beq rkCbm
	lda keyA
	and keyB
	and keyC
	and keyE
	and keyF
	and #KD_CBM
	beq rkNoCbm
rkCbm	inc fireDown
rkNoCbm
	rts

; The mouse: turn by POT X, look up and down by POT Y, from what mouseIrq
; has gathered since the last frame, both scaled by the controls menu's
; MOUSE SPEED. Taken while dead too, and dropped, like the keys
; (deadKeys). An odd POT Y count stays behind for the next frame, so slow
; movement is halved evenly in both directions.
mouseLook
	php
	sei
	lda accX
	sta mdLo
	lda accX+1
	sta mdHi
	lda accY
	sta mdY
	and #1
	sta accY
	lda #0
	sta accX
	sta accX+1
	plp
	lda plDead
	bne mlDone

	lda mdHi			; |counts| <= MOUSE_YCAP
	beq mlPos
	cmp #$ff
	bne mlSat
	lda mdLo
	cmp #256-MOUSE_YCAP
	bcs mlYaw
	bcc mlSat
mlPos
	lda mdLo
	cmp #MOUSE_YCAP+1
	bcc mlYaw
mlSat
	lda mdHi
	bmi +
	lda #MOUSE_YCAP
	ldx #0
	beq ++
+	lda #256-MOUSE_YCAP
	ldx #$ff
+	sta mdLo
	stx mdHi
mlYaw
	ldx mouseSens			; yaw += counts * msYaw, modulo a turn
	lda msYaw,x
	jsr msMul16
	clc
	lda camYaw
	adc msRLo
	sta camYaw
	lda camYaw+1
	adc msRHi
	sta camYaw+1

	jsr msPitch			; mdY, scaled: the change in pitch
	clc
	adc camPitch			; 63 + PITCH_LIM < 128: no overflow
	bmi mlNeg
	cmp #PITCH_LIM+1
	bcc mlSet
	lda #PITCH_LIM
	bne mlSet
mlNeg
	cmp #256-PITCH_LIM
	bcs mlSet
	lda #256-PITCH_LIM
mlSet
	sta camPitch
mlDone
	rts
	.cerror PITCH_LIM > 64, "mouseLook's pitch sum can overflow"


; One sample, from sfxIrq on every IRQ. Runs with the IRQ's registers
; saved; uses A, X, Y.
mouseIrq
	ldy potSkip			; readKeys' verdict on this sample
	lda #0
	sta potSkip
	lda mouseSeen
	beq miDone
	tya
	bne miDone
	lda POT_X
	sta potX
	lda POT_Y
	sta potY
	and potX
	cmp #$ff
	beq miDone			; port 1 off, or no mouse: both read $ff
	lda potSync
	beq miDelta
	lda #0
	sta potSync
	lda potX
	sta potOld
	lda potY
	sta potOld+1
miDone
	rts
miDelta
	ldx #0
	lda potX
	jsr mouseDelta
	jsr mouseGuard
	jsr mouseAccel
	ldy #0
	cmp #$80
	bcc +
	dey
+	clc
	adc accX
	sta accX
	tya
	adc accX+1
	sta accX+1
	ldx #1
	lda potY
	jsr mouseDelta
	clc
	adc accY
	bvc +				; saturates: a pitch past the limit anyway
	lda #$7f
	bit accY
	bpl +
	lda #$80
+	sta accY
	rts

; A = signed X counts of one sample, -32..31. Returns them accelerated,
; -88..88, in A with the flags of A.
mouseAccel
	sta maSign
	bpl +
	eor #$ff
	clc
	adc #1
+	cmp #MOUSE_ACC_T+1
	bcc maSigned
	sta maTmp			; |d| + 2(|d| - T) = 3|d| - 2T
	asl a
	adc maTmp			; |d| <= 32: the asl left C clear
	sec
	sbc #2*MOUSE_ACC_T
maSigned
	bit maSign
	bpl +
	eor #$ff
	clc
	adc #1
+	ora #0
	rts

; A = signed X counts of one sample. A sample that reverses the previous
; one outright, both at least about MOUSE_REV counts, is taken for a wrap:
; a hand cannot turn a fast move round within one sample, but a move
; just past 31 counts reads as exactly that. The previous counts stand in
; for it, at most MOUSE_HOLD times running: a flick past the limit keeps
; turning the way it went, and a real reversal is late by that many
; samples (133 ms) at worst and never refused -- one that slows down on
; the way, as a hand does, is not late at all.
mouseGuard
	sta mgCur
	eor mgPrev
	bpl mgTake			; the same way
	lda mgCur
	bpl +
	eor #$ff			; |counts| - 1
+	cmp #MOUSE_REV
	bcc mgTake
	lda mgPrev
	bpl +
	eor #$ff
+	cmp #MOUSE_REV
	bcc mgTake
	lda mgRun
	cmp #MOUSE_HOLD
	bcs mgTake
	inc mgRun
	lda mgPrev
	rts
mgTake
	lda #0
	sta mgRun
	lda mgCur
	sta mgPrev
	rts

; Whatever moved before now is forgotten, and the next sample is only the
; reference. sfxInit calls it: while the IRQ was out, nothing was sampled.
mouseReset
	php
	sei
	lda #1
	sta potSync
	lda #0
	sta potSkip
	sta mgPrev
	sta mgRun
	sta accX
	sta accX+1
	sta accY
	plp
	rts

; The 1351's own recipe. A = the new pot reading, X = 0 (X) or 1 (Y).
; Bits 6-1 carry the position modulo 64 and bit 0 is noise, so the
; difference is taken in seven bits, and its top bit is the sign. Returns
; the signed movement in A; a reading that moved nothing is not kept, so
; the noise bit cannot creep.
mouseDelta
	sta potNew
	sec
	sbc potOld,x
	and #%01111111
	cmp #%01000000
	bcs mdNeg
	lsr a
	beq mdNone
	bne mdKeep
mdNeg
	ora #%11000000
	cmp #$ff
	beq mdNone
	sec
	ror a
mdKeep
	tay
	lda potNew
	sta potOld,x
	tya
	rts
mdNone
	lda #0
	rts

; Freeze diagnostics. A BRK -- which is what a derailed 6502 usually runs
; into, and what used to drop this program to READY -- prints BRK and its
; address on row 0 and stops there. RESTORE prints NMI, the address the
; 6502 was interrupted at and a count of presses, then carries on: pressed
; a few times on a frozen screen, it says which loop the program is stuck
; in. No answer to RESTORE at all means the 6502 is not running.
trapInit
	lda #<brkTrap
	sta $0316
	lda #>brkTrap
	sta $0317
	lda #<nmiTrap
	sta $0318
	lda #>nmiTrap
	sta $0319
	rts

; Reached through the KERNAL's IRQ entry, which has pushed A, X and Y on
; top of BRK's P and return address; the return address is the BRK + 2.
brkTrap
	tsx
	sec
	lda $0105,x
	sbc #2
	sta trapPc
	lda $0106,x
	sbc #0
	sta trapPc+1
	ldy #0
	jsr trapShow
-	jmp -

; The KERNAL's NMI entry is SEI; JMP ($0318), nothing pushed, so after
; the three pushes here the return address sits where brkTrap finds it.
nmiTrap
	pha
	txa
	pha
	tya
	pha
	tsx
	lda $0105,x
	sta trapPc
	lda $0106,x
	sta trapPc+1
	inc trapN
	ldy #3
	jsr trapShow
	pla
	tay
	pla
	tax
	pla
	rti

trapShow
	ldx #0
-	lda trapTag,y
	sta $0400,x
	lda #1
	sta $d800,x
	iny
	inx
	cpx #3
	bne -
	lda #$20
	sta $0403
	sta $0408
	ldx #4
	lda trapPc+1
	jsr trapHex
	lda trapPc
	jsr trapHex
	ldx #9
	lda trapN
trapHex
	pha
	lsr a
	lsr a
	lsr a
	lsr a
	jsr trapNyb
	pla
	and #$0f
trapNyb
	cmp #10
	bcc +
	sbc #9				; carry set: 10-15 -> screen codes A-F
	bne ++
+	ora #$30
+	sta $0400,x
	lda #1
	sta $d800,x
	inx
	rts

trapTag		.byte 2, 18, 11, 14, 13, 9	; BRK, NMI in screen codes
trapPc		.word 0
trapN		.byte 0

mcWalk
	lda swimming
	beq +
	lda camPitch			; a swimmer goes where they look
	ldx #CAM_Y
	jsr camAdd
+	lda camYaw+1
	ldx #CAM_X
	jsr camAdd
	lda camYaw+1
	clc
	adc #64
	ldx #CAM_Z
	jmp camAdd

mcStrafe
	lda camYaw+1
	clc
	adc #64
	ldx #CAM_X
	jsr camAdd
	lda dirNeg
	eor #1
	sta dirNeg
	lda camYaw+1
	ldx #CAM_Z
	jmp camAdd

camAdd
	stx camOff
	tax
	lda sinLo,x
	sta mLo
	lda sinHi,x
	sta mHi

	ldy #0
	lda mHi
	bpl caPos
	iny
	lda #0
	sec
	sbc mLo
	sta mLo
	lda #0
	sbc mHi
	sta mHi
caPos
	tya
	eor dirNeg
	sta caNeg

	lda #0
	sta prod0
	sta prod1
	sta prod2
	lda stepLo
	sta mult
	ldx #8
caMul
	lsr mult
	bcc caNoAdd
	clc
	lda prod1
	adc mLo
	sta prod1
	lda prod2
	adc mHi
	sta prod2
caNoAdd
	ror prod2
	ror prod1
	ror prod0
	dex
	bne caMul

	ldx camOff
	lda caNeg
	bne caSub
	clc
	lda mov+0,x
	adc prod0
	sta mov+0,x
	lda mov+1,x
	adc prod1
	sta mov+1,x
	lda mov+2,x
	adc prod2
	sta mov+2,x
	lda mov+3,x
	adc #0
	sta mov+3,x
	rts
caSub
	sec
	lda mov+0,x
	sbc prod0
	sta mov+0,x
	lda mov+1,x
	sbc prod1
	sta mov+1,x
	lda mov+2,x
	sbc prod2
	sta mov+2,x
	lda mov+3,x
	sbc #0
	sta mov+3,x
	rts

sendCamera
	lda #<CAM_ID
	sta stId
	lda #>CAM_ID
	sta stId+1

	ldx #11
-	lda cam,x
	sta stBuf,x
	dex
	bpl -
	jsr camDrop
	lda #OP_SET_POSITION
	ldx #11
	jsr stage2
	sta posErr

	lda camYaw
	sta stBuf+0
	lda camYaw+1
	sta stBuf+1
	lda #0				; pitch: the wire's is negated, see
	sta stBuf+2			;   PITCH_LIM
	sta stBuf+4
	sta stBuf+5
	sec
	sbc camPitch
	sta stBuf+3
	lda #OP_SET_ORIENTATION
	ldx #5
	jsr stage2
	ora posErr
	sta posErr
	rts

; F1 / F3 look up / down and F7 levels the view; + / - widen / narrow the
; field of view. Both repeat while held, one step a frame. Pitch goes out
; with the camera every frame anyway; the FOV is sent by sendView, once per
; change, and after that by the refresh ring like everything else.
viewKeys
	lda keyA
	and #KA_F7
	bne vkNoLevel
	lda #0
	sta camPitch
vkNoLevel
	lda keyA
	and #KA_F1
	bne vkNoUp
	lda camPitch
	clc
	adc pitchStep
	bmi +				; still below level
	cmp #PITCH_LIM+1
	bcc +
	lda #PITCH_LIM
+	sta camPitch
vkNoUp
	lda keyA
	and #KA_F3
	bne vkNoDown
	lda camPitch
	sec
	sbc pitchStep
	bpl +				; still above level
	cmp #256-PITCH_LIM
	bcs +
	lda #256-PITCH_LIM
+	sta camPitch
vkNoDown
	; F5: telemetry, once per press, after TELE_HOLD frames of it.
	lda keyA
	and #KA_F5
	beq +
	lda #0
	sta teleHold
	beq vkTeleDone
+	lda teleHold
	cmp #TELE_HOLD
	beq vkTeleDone			; acted; wait for the release
	inc teleHold
	lda teleHold
	cmp #TELE_HOLD
	bne vkTeleDone
	lda teleOn
	eor #1
	sta teleOn
	jsr teleShown
vkTeleDone
	lda keyE
	and #KE_PLUS | KE_MINUS
	cmp #KE_PLUS | KE_MINUS
	bne +
	lda #0				; neither held
	sta fovHold
	rts
+	inc fovHold
	lda fovHold
	cmp #FOV_HOLD
	beq +
	rts
+	lda #( FOV_HOLD - FOV_REPEAT ) & $ff	; the next step FOV_REPEAT frames on
	sta fovHold
	lda keyE
	and #KE_PLUS
	bne vkNoWider
	ldx fovIx
	cpx #FOV_N-1
	bcs vkNoWider
	inc fovIx
	lda #1
	sta fovDirty
vkNoWider
	lda keyE
	and #KE_MINUS
	bne vkNoNarrower
	lda fovIx
	beq vkNoNarrower
	dec fovIx
	lda #1
	sta fovDirty
vkNoNarrower
	rts

sendView
	lda fovDirty
	beq +
	lda #0
	sta fovDirty
	jsr rgPersp
	sta fovErr
+	rts

; The firmware raises FRAME_READY in two places only: in the flip's own
; hold at the vsync, and at the top of every dispatch. A frame that core 1
; finishes after its flip has gone out is therefore invisible to a program
; that only reads STATUS -- the 2026-09-08 fix covers the other case. Once
; the second speed pass made the C64 arrive here before a heavy view had
; finished, that cost a whole READY_WAIT (~0.7 s) per stall, looking down
; the first corridor. So while waiting, send one command every WR_PUMP_MS:
; SET_BACKGROUND 0, which the loop does not drain for, changes nothing,
; and carries the check like everything else stage2 sends.
WR_PUMP_MS = 2

waitReady
	lda #<READY_WAIT
	sta waitCt
	lda #>READY_WAIT
	sta waitCt+1
	lda CIA2_TBLO
	sta wrMs
wrPoll
	lda STATUS
	and #( ST_FRAME_READY | ST_BUSY )
	cmp #ST_FRAME_READY
	beq wrDone
	lda wrMs			; the clock counts down
	sec
	sbc CIA2_TBLO
	cmp #WR_PUMP_MS
	bcc wrNoPump
	jsr rgBackground
	inc wrPumps
	bne wrPumped
	inc wrPumps+1
wrPumped
	lda CIA2_TBLO
	sta wrMs
wrNoPump
	lda waitCt
	bne +
	dec waitCt+1
+	dec waitCt
	lda waitCt
	ora waitCt+1
	bne wrPoll

	inc stalls
	bne +
	inc stalls+1
+
	lda stalls
	cmp #1
	bne wrDone
	lda stalls+1
	bne wrDone
	lda frames
	sta stallAt
	lda frames+1
	sta stallAt+1
wrDone
	rts

; ======================================================================
; The state refresh ring.
;
; The first bench run of this program walked E1M1 perfectly for a few
; seconds, then the view jumped and the field of view went wrong; later the
; palette went, and the level turned to flat blue-grey. None of that was the
; program's own doing. It was bus damage to state this program set ONCE, at
; start-up, and then never mentioned again:
;
;   * A command byte that arrives with one bit flipped is a different
;     command. CMD_HI 1 -> 0 makes class 1's SET_POSITION ($30) class 0's
;     PAL_SET, and SET_ORIENTATION ($31) class 0's PAL_LOAD: the camera's
;     own bytes, written into the palette. tools/prgsim reproduces it with
;     --bus-fault=data:N.
;   * A flipped CMD_LO turns one class-1 opcode into another, and the
;     arguments still staged from the last command are very often valid
;     for it -- which is how a field of view changes by itself.
;   * A flipped ID byte sends the camera's position, or a door's, to a
;     chunk of the level.
;
; Each is permanent on a retained scene, because nothing this program does
; per frame would ever contradict it. So the defence is expiry, exactly as in
; gpu64_demo_quake3d.a and docs/state-refresh.md: every piece of state is
; re-sent on a rotation whether or not anything looks wrong. Two rings run
; side by side, one step of each a frame:
;
;   rgTab     the scene-wide state, one entry a frame -- the view, the
;             light, the active camera, the palette, the readback fence
;   nodeRefresh  one LEVEL node a frame, all four of its fields
;
; The palette is the one that needed firmware: its truth lives in the REU
; level pack, not in C64 RAM, so LEVEL_PALETTE ($17) asks the Pi to compare
; its live palette against the level's and put back whatever differs.
; ======================================================================
doRefresh
	ldx rgIx
	lda rgTab,x
	sta rgCall+1
	lda rgTab+1,x
	sta rgCall+2
	inx
	inx
	cpx #RG_N*2
	bcc +
	ldx #0
+	stx rgIx
rgCall	jsr $ffff			; patched above
	beq +
	inc rgErrs
	bne +
	dec rgErrs			; saturates at 255
+	jmp nodeRefresh

rgTab
	.word rgViewport
	.word rgPalette
	.word rgPersp
	.word rgActiveCam
	.word rgBackground
	.word rgPalette			; twice a cycle: it is what a phantom
	.word rgLight			;   hits most often, and the most visible
	.word rgDmaWin
RG_N = ( * - rgTab ) / 2

; Every entry returns the ERRCODE, flags set, and stages all of its own
; arguments: ARG is not cleared between commands, and a leftover byte is
; exactly the kind of state this ring exists to distrust. Every class 1
; entry goes through stage2 and so carries the check: at one flipped byte in
; 61, runsim showed the ring's own SET_ACTIVE_CAMERA arriving as
; CREATE_CAMERA and putting the eye at the origin for a frame -- the ring
; that repairs damage was itself the commonest source of it.
stb	.macro	off, val
	lda #\val
	sta stBuf+\off
	.endm
stw	.macro	off, val
	lda #<(\val)
	sta stBuf+\off
	lda #>(\val)
	sta stBuf+\off+1
	.endm

rgViewport
	#stw 0, 0
	#stw 2, 0
	#stw 4, VP_W
	#stw 6, VP_H
	lda #OP_SET_VIEWPORT
	ldx #7
	jmp stage2

rgPersp
	lda fovIx
	asl a
	tax
	lda fovTab,x
	sta stBuf+0
	lda fovTab+1,x
	sta stBuf+1
	#stw 2, NEARZ
	#stw 4, FARZ
	lda #OP_SET_PERSPECTIVE
	ldx #5
	jmp stage2

rgBackground
	#stb 0, 0
	lda #OP_SET_BACKGROUND
	ldx #0
	jmp stage2

; BAD_ID means CAM_ID is no longer a camera -- a phantom CREATE_OBJECT or
; DESTROY_NODE got it, and the loop has been rendering NO_CAMERA or a stale
; view since. Make it a camera again and put it where the player is before
; this frame is committed; any other error is left to the next time round.
rgActiveCam
	jsr stCamId
	lda #OP_SET_ACTIVE_CAMERA
	ldx #$ff
	jsr stage2
	cmp #ERR_BAD_ID
	beq +
	cmp #0
	rts
+	jsr stCamId
	lda #OP_CREATE_CAMERA
	ldx #$ff
	jsr stage2
	jsr stCamId
	lda #OP_SET_ACTIVE_CAMERA
	ldx #$ff
	jsr stage2
	inc camFix
	bne +
	dec camFix
+	jsr sendCamera
	lda #ERR_BAD_ID			; still counted as the ring's error
	rts

; The state default, towards the viewer, with gpu64's default ambient.
rgLight
	#stw 0, 0
	#stw 2, 0
	#stw 4, -32767
	#stb 6, 4
	lda #OP_SET_LIGHT3D
	ldx #6
	jmp stage2

stCamId
	lda #<CAM_ID
	sta stId
	lda #>CAM_ID
	sta stId+1
	rts

; RESULT is how many entries the Pi had to put back. A read of it that fails
; returns the last bus value, so it is believed only when two reads agree.
rgPalette
	lda #OP_LEVEL_PALETTE
	ldx #$ff
	jsr stage2
	sta palErr
	bne rpDone
	lda RESULT
	cmp RESULT
	bne rpDone
	cmp #0
	beq rpDone
	sta palLast
	inc palFixes
	bne rpDone
	inc palFixes+1
rpDone
	lda palErr
	rts

rgDmaWin
	jsr setDmaWin
	lda fenceEcho
	cmp #FENCE_ECHO
	beq +
	lda #$ff
	rts
+	lda #0
	rts

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; The level's nodes.
;
; LOAD_LEVEL builds every chunk of E1M1 as a node, NODE_BASE upwards, with a
; mesh and a position out of the level file -- so this program never knew
; either and cannot re-send them. It does not need to: LEVEL_NODE ($1C) asks
; the Pi to put one node back the way the file says. What the ring is
; defending against is a phantom CREATE_*: one flipped CMD_LO bit makes a
; SET_POSITION a CREATE_OBJECT, and CREATE_* replaces a live id by design,
; so a flipped ID byte as well leaves a wall that is a camera, a sprite, or
; an instance of a mesh nobody uploaded -- until something puts it back.
;
; Movers' nodes are marked and their positions left to sendMovers, whose own
; ring re-sends them from where the door actually is -- the file's value is
; the CLOSED position and would fight an open door. Nothing else is known
; up front: the ring finds the end of the node run by the BAD_ID it gets
; for the first id past it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
LN_MOVER  = $01

nodeMark
	lda #0
	sta lnCount
	tax
-	sta lnFlag,x
	inx
	bne -

	ldy #0
nmLoop
	cpy nMov
	bcs nmDone
	lda mvNodeC,y
	beq nmNext
	sta lnK
	sec
	lda mvNodeL,y
	sbc #<NODE_BASE
	tax
	lda mvNodeH,y
	sbc #>NODE_BASE
	bne nmNext			; below NODE_BASE, or past a byte
nmNode
	lda #LN_MOVER
	sta lnFlag,x
	inx
	beq nmNext
	dec lnK
	bne nmNode
nmNext
	iny
	jmp nmLoop
nmDone
	rts

; One level node a frame. ARG0 bit 0 asks for the file's position too,
; which a mover must not get. RESULT says what was wrong -- bit 0 the node
; itself, bit 1 its position -- and is believed when two reads agree.
nodeRefresh
	ldx lnIx
	lda lnFlag,x
	eor #LN_MOVER			; 1 for a static node, 0 for a mover
	and #LN_MOVER
	sta stBuf+0
	clc
	txa
	adc #<NODE_BASE
	sta stId
	lda #>NODE_BASE
	adc #0
	sta stId+1
	lda #OP_LEVEL_NODE
	ldx #0
	jsr stage2
	beq nrOk
	cmp #ERR_BAD_ID
	bne nrErr
	; Past the last node: that is where the run ends. An id that was
	; itself mis-sampled can end a sweep early, which costs nothing but
	; a shorter sweep, so the count only ever grows.
	lda lnIx
	cmp lnCount
	bcc +
	sta lnCount
+	lda #0
	sta lnIx
	rts
nrErr
	inc rgErrs
	bne nrNext
	dec rgErrs
	jmp nrNext
nrOk
	lda RESULT
	cmp RESULT
	bne nrNext
	cmp #0
	beq nrNext
	sta lnLast
	inc lnFix
	bne nrNext
	dec lnFix			; saturates at 255
nrNext
	inc lnIx
	rts

; The field of view as a binary angle, FOV_DEG0 upwards in FOV_STEP degrees.
fovTab
	.for i = 0, i < FOV_N, i += 1
	.word round( ( FOV_DEG0 + i * FOV_STEP ) * 65536.0 / 360.0 )
	.next

; ======================================================================
; THE GAME. Everything below this line runs on the 6502 and sends gpu64
; nothing it could not have sent in milestone 15.
;
; The whole of it is built on two facts about LEVEL_ENT's answer. First,
; target and targetname arrive as opaque 16-bit ids out of a deduplicated
; string pool, so "does this button open this door" is a 16-bit compare and
; there is not one byte of string handling here. Second, a brush model's
; nodes are a contiguous RUN of scene ids starting at nodeFirst, and the
; node is placed at the entity's own origin -- so a door is animated by
; sending origin + travel * frac to nodeFirst..nodeFirst+nodeCount-1 and
; nothing ever has to be looked up or read back.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; The scan. One LEVEL_ENT per entity, three tries each, and the level's own
; entity count comes back in every answer -- which is why the loop can start
; without knowing how long it is.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
entScan
	jsr mtClear
	lda #0
	sta nMov
	sta nTrg
	sta nTel
	sta nCnt
	sta secTotal
	sta entBad
	sta entDrop
	sta entIdx
	sta entIdx+1
	sta entTot+1
	lda #1				; one, until the first answer says
	sta entTot

esLoop
	lda entIdx
	and #$1f
	bne esNoShow
	lda #3
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "ENTS    "
	.byte 0
	lda entIdx+1
	jsr dmHexA
	lda entIdx
	jsr dmHexA
esNoShow

	jsr entRead
	bne esBad
	jsr entTake
	jmp esNext
esBad
	lda entBad
	cmp #255
	beq esNext
	inc entBad
esNext
	inc entIdx
	bne +
	inc entIdx+1
+
	lda entIdx+1
	cmp entTot+1
	bcc esLoop
	bne esDone
	lda entIdx
	cmp entTot
	bcc esLoop
esDone
	rts

; entBlk holds a verified record, and A = 0, or the record was unreadable
; three times running and A = $FF. The magic AND the checksum, as every
; block readback in this program does -- a checksum alone accepts an
; all-zero block, which is exactly what an answer that never arrived looks
; like once the poison is gone.
entRead
	ldx #3
erTry
	stx erCount

	lda entIdx
	sta entBlk+ENT_IDX
	lda entIdx+1
	sta entBlk+ENT_IDX+1
	lda #ENT_IN_M
	sta entBlk+ENT_MAGIC
	lda entBlk+ENT_IDX
	eor entBlk+ENT_IDX+1
	eor entBlk+ENT_MAGIC
	sta entBlk+ENT_CHECK

	lda #$a5
	ldx #ENT_OUT
-	sta entBlk,x
	inx
	cpx #ENT_LEN
	bne -

	#argblob 0, SPACE_C64, entBlk, ENT_LEN
	lda #OP_LEVEL_ENT
	jsr cmd1
	bne erRetry

	lda entBlk+ENT_OMAGIC
	cmp #ENT_OUT_M
	bne erRetry
	lda #0
	ldx #ENT_OUT
-	eor entBlk,x
	inx
	cpx #ENT_OCHECK
	bne -
	cmp entBlk+ENT_OCHECK
	bne erRetry

	lda entBlk+ENT_ECOUNT
	sta entTot
	lda entBlk+ENT_ECOUNT+1
	sta entTot+1
	lda #0
	rts
erRetry
	ldx erCount
	dex
	bne erTry
	lda #$ff
	rts

; Sort the record into one of the two tables, or drop it. The test is on
; the KIND RANGES and not on particular kinds: a level with a class this
; program has never heard of still lands in the right half, which is the
; whole reason gpu64_level.h digests classnames into ranges.
entTake
	lda entBlk+ENT_KIND
	cmp #K_BANK
	bne +
	jmp mtBank			; a level's monster frames
+	cmp #K_MOVER_1
	bcc etNo
	cmp #K_MOVER_2+1
	bcs +
	jmp etMover
+	cmp #K_TRIG_1
	bcc etNo
	cmp #K_TRIG_2+1
	bcs +
	jmp etTrig
+	jmp actTake			; items and monsters
etNo
	rts

; A mover is a brush model with somewhere to go. func_wall is in the same
; kind range and has a travel of zero, so this is also what keeps the
; furniture out of the table.
etMover
	lda entBlk+ENT_MODEL
	bne +
	jmp etNo
+	lda #0
	ldx #ENT_TRAVEL
-	ora entBlk,x
	inx
	cpx #ENT_TRAVEL+12
	bne -
	cmp #0				; the flags above belong to cpx, not to A
	bne +
	jmp etNo
+
	ldx nMov
	cpx #MAX_MOV
	bcc +
	jmp etDrop
+

	lda entBlk+ENT_MODEL
	sta mvModel,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_NODEF
	sta mvNodeL,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_NODEF+1
	sta mvNodeH,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_NODEC
	sta mvNodeC,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_KIND
	sta mvKind,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_TNAME
	sta mvTnL,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_TNAME+1
	sta mvTnH,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_TARGET
	sta mvTgL,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_TARGET+1
	sta mvTgH,x
	lda #MV_CLOSED
	sta mvState,x
	lda #0
	sta mvFrac,x
	sta mvWait,x
	sta mvDirty,x
	jsr exStayTake			; wait -1: stays open

	lda entBlk+ENT_ORIGIN+0
	sta mvBX0,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_ORIGIN+1
	sta mvBX1,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_ORIGIN+2
	sta mvBX2,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_ORIGIN+3
	sta mvBX3,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_ORIGIN+4
	sta mvBY0,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_ORIGIN+5
	sta mvBY1,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_ORIGIN+6
	sta mvBY2,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_ORIGIN+7
	sta mvBY3,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_ORIGIN+8
	sta mvBZ0,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_ORIGIN+9
	sta mvBZ1,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_ORIGIN+10
	sta mvBZ2,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_ORIGIN+11
	sta mvBZ3,x

	lda entBlk+ENT_TRAVEL+0
	sta mvTX0,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_TRAVEL+1
	sta mvTX1,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_TRAVEL+2
	sta mvTX2,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_TRAVEL+3
	sta mvTX3,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_TRAVEL+4
	sta mvTY0,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_TRAVEL+5
	sta mvTY1,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_TRAVEL+6
	sta mvTY2,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_TRAVEL+7
	sta mvTY3,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_TRAVEL+8
	sta mvTZ0,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_TRAVEL+9
	sta mvTZ1,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_TRAVEL+10
	sta mvTZ2,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_TRAVEL+11
	sta mvTZ3,x

	jsr mulOfs			; frac 0: none, or a START_OPEN door's all

	; The box, in fine units: the two middle bytes of each 16.16 value and
	; no arithmetic at all. This is the CLOSED box, and it stays the closed
	; box while the door moves -- which is right, because Quake's own door
	; trigger field is built once from the closed panel and does not travel
	; with it either.
	lda entBlk+ENT_MINS+1
	sta mvMnXl,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_MINS+2
	sta mvMnXh,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_MINS+5
	sta mvMnYl,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_MINS+6
	sta mvMnYh,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_MINS+9
	sta mvMnZl,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_MINS+10
	sta mvMnZh,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_MAXS+1
	sta mvMxXl,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_MAXS+2
	sta mvMxXh,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_MAXS+5
	sta mvMxYl,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_MAXS+6
	sta mvMxYh,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_MAXS+9
	sta mvMxZl,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_MAXS+10
	sta mvMxZh,x

	inc nMov
	rts

etDrop
	lda entDrop
	cmp #255
	beq +
	inc entDrop
+	rts

; A trigger volume is its box and its target, and nothing else. Half of
; E1M1's brush models are these, and the converter draws nothing for them
; -- nodeCount is 0 and that is not an error.
etTrig
	lda entBlk+ENT_KIND
	cmp #K_TRIG_COUNTER
	bne +
	jmp cntTake			; a counter is not a volume at all
+	lda entBlk+ENT_MODEL
	bne +
	jmp etNo
+	ldx nTrg
	cpx #MAX_TRG
	bcc +
	jmp etDrop
+

	lda entBlk+ENT_P1		; trigger_changelevel's slot + 1
	sta trDest,x
	jsr tpTake			; ... or a teleport's
	lda entBlk+ENT_KIND
	sta trKind,x
	cmp #K_TRIG_SECRET
	bne +
	inc secTotal
+	lda entBlk+ENT_TARGET
	sta trTgL,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_TARGET+1
	sta trTgH,x
	lda #0
	sta trFired,x

	lda entBlk+ENT_MINS+1
	sta trMnXl,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_MINS+2
	sta trMnXh,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_MINS+5
	sta trMnYl,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_MINS+6
	sta trMnYh,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_MINS+9
	sta trMnZl,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_MINS+10
	sta trMnZh,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_MAXS+1
	sta trMxXl,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_MAXS+2
	sta trMxXh,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_MAXS+5
	sta trMxYl,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_MAXS+6
	sta trMxYh,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_MAXS+9
	sta trMxZl,x
	lda entBlk+ENT_MAXS+10
	sta trMxZh,x

	inc nTrg
	rts

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Where the player is, in fine units. The middle two bytes of a 16.16
; coordinate ARE the position in 1/256 world units, signed, and the whole
; of E1M1 fits in sixteen bits of them.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
pfCalc
	lda cam+CAM_X+1
	sta pfX
	lda cam+CAM_X+2
	sta pfX+1
	lda cam+CAM_Y+1
	sta pfY
	lda cam+CAM_Y+2
	sta pfY+1
	lda cam+CAM_Z+1
	sta pfZ
	lda cam+CAM_Z+2
	sta pfZ+1
	eor #$80
	sta pfZb
	lda pfX+1
	eor #$80
	sta pfXb
	rts

; X = mover index. C set if its box is at least fxR whole units clear of the
; player on x or z, read off the high bytes alone -- the prefilter in front
; of the full box work. Signed bytes are compared biased by $80. Preserves X.
mvFar
	lda mvMnXh,x		; box min - player
	eor #$80
	sec
	sbc pfXb
	bcc +
	cmp fxR
	bcs mvfYes
+	lda mvMxXh,x		; player - box max
	eor #$80
	sta fxT
	lda pfXb
	sec
	sbc fxT
	bcc +
	cmp fxR
	bcs mvfYes
+
	lda mvMnZh,x		; box min - player
	eor #$80
	sec
	sbc pfZb
	bcc +
	cmp fxR
	bcs mvfYes
+	lda mvMxZh,x		; player - box max
	eor #$80
	sta fxT
	lda pfZb
	sec
	sbc fxT
	bcc +
	cmp fxR
	bcs mvfYes
+
	clc
	rts
mvfYes
	sec
	rts

; X = trigger index. C set if its box is at least fxR whole units clear of the
; player on x or z, read off the high bytes alone -- the prefilter in front
; of the full box work. Signed bytes are compared biased by $80. Preserves X.
trFar
	lda trMnXh,x		; box min - player
	eor #$80
	sec
	sbc pfXb
	bcc +
	cmp fxR
	bcs trfYes
+	lda trMxXh,x		; player - box max
	eor #$80
	sta fxT
	lda pfXb
	sec
	sbc fxT
	bcc +
	cmp fxR
	bcs trfYes
+
	lda trMnZh,x		; box min - player
	eor #$80
	sec
	sbc pfZb
	bcc +
	cmp fxR
	bcs trfYes
+	lda trMxZh,x		; player - box max
	eor #$80
	sta fxT
	lda pfZb
	sec
	sbc fxT
	bcc +
	cmp fxR
	bcs trfYes
+
	clc
	rts
trfYes
	sec
	rts

; X = mover / trigger index; loads its box into the six scratch pairs the
; two box tests read. Two loaders and one test, rather than a test that
; knows about both tables.
mvLoadBox
	lda mvMnXl,x
	sta bxMnX
	lda mvMnXh,x
	sta bxMnX+1
	lda mvMnYl,x
	sta bxMnY
	lda mvMnYh,x
	sta bxMnY+1
	lda mvMnZl,x
	sta bxMnZ
	lda mvMnZh,x
	sta bxMnZ+1
	lda mvMxXl,x
	sta bxMxX
	lda mvMxXh,x
	sta bxMxX+1
	lda mvMxYl,x
	sta bxMxY
	lda mvMxYh,x
	sta bxMxY+1
	lda mvMxZl,x
	sta bxMxZ
	lda mvMxZh,x
	sta bxMxZ+1
	rts

trLoadBox
	lda trMnXl,x
	sta bxMnX
	lda trMnXh,x
	sta bxMnX+1
	lda trMnYl,x
	sta bxMnY
	lda trMnYh,x
	sta bxMnY+1
	lda trMnZl,x
	sta bxMnZ
	lda trMnZh,x
	sta bxMnZ+1
	lda trMxXl,x
	sta bxMxX
	lda trMxXh,x
	sta bxMxX+1
	lda trMxYl,x
	sta bxMxY
	lda trMxYh,x
	sta bxMxY+1
	lda trMxZl,x
	sta bxMxZ
	lda trMxZh,x
	sta bxMxZ+1
	rts

; A = how far the player is from the box, in WHOLE world units, clamped to
; 255 and 0 when inside. Only the high byte of each axis gap is used: this
; is a ranking key for "which sixteen movers", not a distance, and a door
; that rounds down half a unit is still the same door.
;
; The subtractions are plain 16-bit and the sign bit of the result is read
; directly, with no overflow handling: every fine coordinate in a level this
; format can express is inside +-32767, and so is every difference of two of
; them that matters here.
boxScore
	lda #0
	sta bsSum
	sta bsSum+1

	sec
	lda bxMnX
	sbc pfX
	lda bxMnX+1
	sbc pfX+1
	bpl +
	sec
	lda pfX
	sbc bxMxX
	lda pfX+1
	sbc bxMxX+1
	bpl +
	lda #0
+
	clc
	adc bsSum
	sta bsSum
	bcc +
	inc bsSum+1
+

	sec
	lda bxMnY
	sbc pfY
	lda bxMnY+1
	sbc pfY+1
	bpl +
	sec
	lda pfY
	sbc bxMxY
	lda pfY+1
	sbc bxMxY+1
	bpl +
	lda #0
+
	clc
	adc bsSum
	sta bsSum
	bcc +
	inc bsSum+1
+

	sec
	lda bxMnZ
	sbc pfZ
	lda bxMnZ+1
	sbc pfZ+1
	bpl +
	sec
	lda pfZ
	sbc bxMxZ
	lda pfZ+1
	sbc bxMxZ+1
	bpl +
	lda #0
+
	clc
	adc bsSum
	sta bsSum
	bcc +
	inc bsSum+1
+
	lda bsSum+1
	beq +
	lda #255
	rts
+	lda bsSum
	rts

; The two expansions. A door's is Quake's own trigger field; a trigger
; volume's is the player's own size, because the volume is already the
; shape the mapper drew.
expDoor
	lda #<TOUCH_XZ
	sta exH
	lda #>TOUCH_XZ
	sta exH+1
	lda #<TOUCH_Y
	sta exYlo
	sta exYhi
	lda #>TOUCH_Y
	sta exYlo+1
	sta exYhi+1
	rts

expTrig
	lda #<TRIG_XZ
	sta exH
	lda #>TRIG_XZ
	sta exH+1
	lda #<TRIG_YLO
	sta exYlo
	lda #>TRIG_YLO
	sta exYlo+1
	lda #<TRIG_YHI
	sta exYhi
	lda #>TRIG_YHI
	sta exYhi+1
	rts

; A = 0 (Z set) if the player is inside the loaded box grown by the loaded
; expansion. Six 16-bit compares and an early exit on the first miss, which
; for a level's worth of boxes is nearly always the first one.
boxInside
	sec
	lda bxMnX
	sbc exH
	sta biLo
	lda bxMnX+1
	sbc exH+1
	sta biHi
	sec
	lda pfX
	sbc biLo
	lda pfX+1
	sbc biHi
	bpl +
	jmp biOut
+
	clc
	lda bxMxX
	adc exH
	sta biLo
	lda bxMxX+1
	adc exH+1
	sta biHi
	sec
	lda biLo
	sbc pfX
	lda biHi
	sbc pfX+1
	bpl +
	jmp biOut
+

	sec
	lda bxMnY
	sbc exYlo
	sta biLo
	lda bxMnY+1
	sbc exYlo+1
	sta biHi
	sec
	lda pfY
	sbc biLo
	lda pfY+1
	sbc biHi
	bpl +
	jmp biOut
+
	clc
	lda bxMxY
	adc exYhi
	sta biLo
	lda bxMxY+1
	adc exYhi+1
	sta biHi
	sec
	lda biLo
	sbc pfY
	lda biHi
	sbc pfY+1
	bpl +
	jmp biOut
+

	sec
	lda bxMnZ
	sbc exH
	sta biLo
	lda bxMnZ+1
	sbc exH+1
	sta biHi
	sec
	lda pfZ
	sbc biLo
	lda pfZ+1
	sbc biHi
	bpl +
	jmp biOut
+
	clc
	lda bxMxZ
	adc exH
	sta biLo
	lda bxMxZ+1
	adc exH+1
	sta biHi
	sec
	lda biLo
	sbc pfZ
	lda biHi
	sbc pfZ+1
	bpl +
	jmp biOut
+

	lda #0
	rts
biOut
	lda #$ff
	rts

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Which movers the trace is given. CLIP_MOVE takes at most sixteen and
; E1M1 has twenty-nine, so the choice is made per frame and it is made by
; distance: the sixteen nearest boxes, kept in a sorted list.
;
; The bound is not a corner cut. A door is only worth tracing against while
; the player can reach it inside one frame's step, and the measured worst
; case on E1M1 is nine movers within sixteen world units of anywhere a
; player can stand. Sorting rather than taking the first sixteen is what
; makes that true of a level nobody has measured.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
gameSelect
	lda wtFrame			; every other frame: the list reaches
	and #1				; NEAR_WU out and a frame's step is
	beq +				; under one unit
	rts
+	lda #1
	sta bmStale
	jsr pfCalc
	lda #NEAR_WU+1			; a high-byte gap this big scores >= NEAR_WU
	sta fxR
	lda #0
	sta selCnt
	sta gsI
gsLoop
	lda gsI
	cmp nMov
	bcs gsDone
	tax
	jsr mvFar
	bcs gsNext
	jsr mvLoadBox
	jsr boxScore
	cmp #NEAR_WU
	bcs gsNext
	sta gsScore

	ldy selCnt
	cpy #SEL_MAX
	bcc gsRoom
	cmp selScore+SEL_MAX-1		; the list is full: is it worth a slot?
	bcs gsNext
	ldy #SEL_MAX-1
	jmp gsHole
gsRoom
	inc selCnt
gsHole
	sty gsHoleIx
gsShift
	ldy gsHoleIx
	beq gsPlace
	dey
	lda selScore,y
	cmp gsScore
	bcc gsPlace			; the one above is nearer: stop here
	ldx gsHoleIx
	sta selScore,x
	lda selIdx,y
	sta selIdx,x
	dec gsHoleIx
	jmp gsShift
gsPlace
	ldx gsHoleIx
	lda gsScore
	sta selScore,x
	lda gsI
	sta selIdx,x
gsNext
	inc gsI
	jmp gsLoop
gsDone
	rts

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; What the player is standing in. Two passes: the trigger volumes, which
; fire their target; and the movers that nothing targets, which open on
; being walked into -- that second rule is Quake's, and it is why an
; ordinary door needs no trigger entity at all.
;
; Every other frame, the ones gameSelect skips: a trigger is grown by the
; player's 16 QU half-width on each side, and two frames of running cover
; 21 QU, so not even a trigger of no thickness can be stepped over. Queued
; fires are still drained every frame.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
gameTouch
	lda wtFrame
	and #1
	bne +
	jmp fireDrain
+	jsr pfCalc
	lda #TOUCH_FAR
	sta fxR

	jsr expDoor
	lda #0
	sta gtI
gtMov
	lda gtI
	cmp nMov
	bcs gtMovDone
	tax
	lda mvTnL,x
	ora mvTnH,x
	bne gtMovNext			; targeted: it waits to be told
	jsr mvFar
	bcs gtMovNext
	jsr mvLoadBox
	jsr boxInside
	bne gtMovNext
	ldx gtI
	jsr openMover
gtMovNext
	inc gtI
	jmp gtMov
gtMovDone

	jsr expTrig
	lda #0
	sta gtI
gtTrg
	lda gtI
	cmp nTrg
	bcs gtTrgDone
	tax
	lda trFired,x
	bne gtTrgNext
	jsr trFar
	bcs gtTrgNext
	jsr trLoadBox
	jsr boxInside
	bne gtTrgNext
	ldx gtI
	lda trKind,x
	jsr exTrig			; secrets, the exit; A = the kind
	cmp #K_TRIG_MULTI
	beq +
	lda #1				; once and secret: never again
	sta trFired,x
+
	ldx gtI
	lda trTgL,x
	ldy trTgH,x
	jsr firePush
gtTrgNext
	inc gtI
	jmp gtTrg
gtTrgDone
	jmp fireDrain

; A = the clip's water level. A splash when the feet go in or come out.
; X and Y survive.
setWater
	sta swNew
	lda plWater
	beq swDry
	lda swNew
	bne swSet			; still wet
	beq swSplash
swDry
	lda swNew
	beq swSet			; still dry
swSplash
	lda #SFX_SPLASH
	jsr sfxPlay
swSet
	lda swNew
	sta plWater
	rts
swNew		.byte 0

; X = mover. Start it opening, and fire whatever IT targets -- a button's
; target is its door, and a door's target is the next door in a chain.
;
; The four states are not four cases here. An already-open mover gets its
; wait restarted, which is what keeps a door open while somebody stands in
; the doorway; one already opening is left alone; and one part-way through
; CLOSING starts opening again from wherever it has got to, which is both
; Quake's behaviour and the only way a door cannot shut on the player.
openMover
	lda mvState,x
	cmp #MV_OPENING
	beq omNo
	cmp #MV_OPEN
	beq omRearm
	lda #MV_OPENING
	sta mvState,x
	lda #SFX_DOOR
	ldy mvKind,x
	cpy #K_BUTTON
	bne +
	lda #SFX_BUTTON
+	jsr sfxPlay
	inc openCt
	bne +
	inc openCt+1
+
	lda mvTgL,x
	ldy mvTgH,x
	jmp firePush
omRearm
	lda #OPEN_WAIT
	sta mvWait,x
omNo
	rts

; A=lo, Y=hi: queue a targetname to fire. Id 0 means "targets nothing" and
; is the common case, so it is the first test.
;
; The queue is what keeps this out of recursion. A door that targets a door
; that targets the first would recurse forever through openMover; pushing
; instead means a chain of any shape costs one pass each, and a cycle stops
; when the queue fills.
firePush
	sta fpLo
	sty fpHi
	ora fpHi
	beq fpNo
	ldx fireN
	cpx #FIRE_Q
	bcs fpNo
	lda fpLo
	sta fireQL,x
	lda fpHi
	sta fireQH,x
	inc fireN
fpNo
	rts

fireDrain
	lda #0
	sta fireRd
fdLoop
	lda fireRd
	cmp fireN
	bcs fdDone
	tax
	lda fireQL,x
	sta fireTL
	sta lastFireL
	lda fireQH,x
	sta fireTH
	sta lastFireH
	inc fireRd
	jsr fireTarget
	jmp fdLoop
fdDone
	lda #0
	sta fireN
	rts

; Open every mover whose targetname is the fired id. Two 8-bit compares per
; mover and no string anywhere: the pool ids did that work once, on the Pi,
; when the level was built.
fireTarget
	lda #0
	sta ftI
ftLoop
	lda ftI
	cmp nMov
	bcs ftDone
	tax
	lda mvTnL,x
	cmp fireTL
	bne ftNext
	lda mvTnH,x
	cmp fireTH
	bne ftNext
	jsr openMover
ftNext
	inc ftI
	jmp ftLoop
ftDone
	jmp cntFire			; and every counter listening for it

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; The state machines. CLOSED -> OPENING -> OPEN (wait) -> CLOSING ->
; CLOSED, one DOOR_STEP of the fraction per frame, and a new ofs computed
; only for the movers that actually moved this frame.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
gameAnimate
	lda #0
	sta aniCt
	sta gaI
gaLoop
	lda gaI
	cmp nMov
	bcs gaDone
	tax
	lda mvState,x
	beq gaNext			; CLOSED and staying that way
	cmp #MV_OPENING
	beq gaOpening
	cmp #MV_OPEN
	beq gaOpen

	lda mvFrac,x			; CLOSING
	sec
	sbc doorStep
	bcs +
	lda #0
+	sta mvFrac,x
	bne gaMoved
	lda #MV_CLOSED
	sta mvState,x
	jmp gaMoved

gaOpening
	lda mvFrac,x
	clc
	adc doorStep
	bcs gaFull
	cmp #255
	bcc gaOpSet
gaFull
	lda #255
	sta mvFrac,x
	lda #MV_OPEN
	sta mvState,x
	lda #OPEN_WAIT
	sta mvWait,x
	jmp gaMoved
gaOpSet
	sta mvFrac,x
	jmp gaMoved

gaOpen
	lda mvStay,x
	lsr a
	bcs gaNext			; wait -1: open for good
	dec mvWait,x
	bne gaNext
	lda #MV_CLOSING
	sta mvState,x
	jmp gaNext

gaMoved
	inc aniCt
	ldx gaI
	jsr mulOfs
	ldx gaI
	lda #1
	sta mvDirty,x
gaNext
	inc gaI
	jmp gaLoop
gaDone
	rts

; X = mover: ofs = travel * frac / 256, per component, signed.
;
; The fraction is a byte and travel is 16.16, so this is a 32x8 shift-add
; whose 40-bit product is taken from bit 8 up -- which is the same thing as
; dividing by 256 and costs nothing. frac 255 lands one 1/256th short of
; the full travel, and a door that stops 1/256 of a world unit from its
; destination is a door that reached it.
mulOfs
	lda #1
	sta bmStale			; the block's copy is out of date
	stx moI
	lda mvFrac,x
	ldy mvStay,x
	bpl +
	eor #$ff			; START_OPEN: closed is all the way out
+	sta moFrac

	lda mvTX0,x
	sta t0
	lda mvTX1,x
	sta t1
	lda mvTX2,x
	sta t2
	lda mvTX3,x
	sta t3
	jsr mulComp
	ldx moI
	lda p1
	sta mvOX0,x
	lda p2
	sta mvOX1,x
	lda p3
	sta mvOX2,x
	lda p4
	sta mvOX3,x

	lda mvTY0,x
	sta t0
	lda mvTY1,x
	sta t1
	lda mvTY2,x
	sta t2
	lda mvTY3,x
	sta t3
	jsr mulComp
	ldx moI
	lda p1
	sta mvOY0,x
	lda p2
	sta mvOY1,x
	lda p3
	sta mvOY2,x
	lda p4
	sta mvOY3,x

	lda mvTZ0,x
	sta t0
	lda mvTZ1,x
	sta t1
	lda mvTZ2,x
	sta t2
	lda mvTZ3,x
	sta t3
	jsr mulComp
	ldx moI
	lda p1
	sta mvOZ0,x
	lda p2
	sta mvOZ1,x
	lda p3
	sta mvOZ2,x
	lda p4
	sta mvOZ3,x
	rts

; t0..t3 (signed 32) * moFrac -> p1..p4, i.e. the product shifted down 8.
; The sign is taken off the front and put back on the answer, because a
; shift-add of a negative multiplicand adds a sign-extension error at every
; step that no amount of later correction removes.
mulComp
	lda #0
	sta moNeg
	sta p0
	sta p1
	sta p2
	sta p3
	sta p4
	lda t3
	bpl +
	inc moNeg
	sec
	lda #0
	sbc t0
	sta t0
	lda #0
	sbc t1
	sta t1
	lda #0
	sbc t2
	sta t2
	lda #0
	sbc t3
	sta t3
+
	lda moFrac
	sta moMul
	ldx #8
mcLoop
	lsr moMul
	bcc mcSkip
	clc
	lda p1
	adc t0
	sta p1
	lda p2
	adc t1
	sta p2
	lda p3
	adc t2
	sta p3
	lda p4
	adc t3
	sta p4
	jmp mcShift			; the carry out of p4 is the bit in
mcSkip
	clc
mcShift
	ror p4
	ror p3
	ror p2
	ror p1
	ror p0
	dex
	bne mcLoop

	lda moNeg
	beq +
	sec
	lda #0
	sbc p1
	sta p1
	lda #0
	sbc p2
	sta p2
	lda #0
	sbc p3
	sta p3
	lda #0
	sbc p4
	sta p4
+	rts

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Put the moved doors where the player will see them. SET_POSITION over
; nodeFirst..nodeFirst+nodeCount-1, and the same ofs went into this frame's
; mover records -- so the door you can see and the door you cannot walk
; through are the same door, to within one frame.
;
; Plus one mover re-sent per frame regardless: a SET_POSITION whose ARG
; bytes were mis-sampled leaves a door stuck at the wrong place forever,
; and the refresh ring is how retained state is repaired in this API.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
sendMovers
	lda #0
	sta movErr
	sta smI
smLoop
	lda smI
	cmp nMov
	bcs smRefresh
	tax
	lda mvDirty,x
	beq smNext
	lda #0
	sta mvDirty,x
	ldx smI
	jsr sendMoverPos
smNext
	inc smI
	jmp smLoop

smRefresh
	lda nMov
	beq smDone
	ldx refreshIx
	inx
	cpx nMov
	bcc +
	ldx #0
+	stx refreshIx
	jsr sendMoverPos
smDone
	rts

; X = mover. The position is computed once into stBuf and staged again for
; every node of the run -- see stage2 for why it is staged at all.
sendMoverPos
	stx spI
	lda mvNodeC,x
	bne +
	rts
+	sta spCount

	clc
	lda mvBX0,x
	adc mvOX0,x
	sta stBuf+0
	lda mvBX1,x
	adc mvOX1,x
	sta stBuf+1
	lda mvBX2,x
	adc mvOX2,x
	sta stBuf+2
	lda mvBX3,x
	adc mvOX3,x
	sta stBuf+3
	clc
	lda mvBY0,x
	adc mvOY0,x
	sta stBuf+4
	lda mvBY1,x
	adc mvOY1,x
	sta stBuf+5
	lda mvBY2,x
	adc mvOY2,x
	sta stBuf+6
	lda mvBY3,x
	adc mvOY3,x
	sta stBuf+7
	clc
	lda mvBZ0,x
	adc mvOZ0,x
	sta stBuf+8
	lda mvBZ1,x
	adc mvOZ1,x
	sta stBuf+9
	lda mvBZ2,x
	adc mvOZ2,x
	sta stBuf+10
	lda mvBZ3,x
	adc mvOZ3,x
	sta stBuf+11

	lda mvNodeL,x
	sta spNode
	lda mvNodeH,x
	sta spNode+1
spNodeLoop
	lda spNode
	sta stId
	sta lastNodeL
	lda spNode+1
	sta stId+1
	sta lastNodeH
	lda #OP_SET_POSITION
	ldx #11
	jsr stage2
	beq +
	lda movErr
	cmp #255
	beq +
	inc movErr
+
	inc spNode
	bne +
	inc spNode+1
+
	dec spCount
	bne spNodeLoop
spDone
	rts

; ======================================================================
; The health sample. Four tries, and the block has to verify itself before
; any of it is believed -- see HB_LEN. hbBad counts the samples that never
; did, saturating, so a row of stale numbers says so instead of looking
; like a Pi that stopped changing.
; ======================================================================
doHealth
	lda #HB_EVERY
	sta hbWait

	; Latch the program's own commit count as of this sample, so the row
	; can print it beside the Pi's flip count. The two are the same number
	; seen from the two ends of the same protocol, and a reader comparing
	; them must not have to allow for the sixty-odd frames since.
	lda okFrames
	sta hbOkAt
	lda okFrames+1
	sta hbOkAt+1

	ldx #4
dhTry
	stx hbTry

	; Poison the trailer, not the body: the body is what the check bytes
	; are computed over, and zeroing it would make a block that never
	; arrived check out perfectly.
	lda #0
	sta healthBuf+HB_MAGIC
	sta healthBuf+HB_MAGIC+1
	sta healthBuf+HB_MAGIC+2
	sta healthBuf+HB_MAGIC+3

	#argblob 0, SPACE_C64, healthBuf, HB_LEN
	lda #OP_GET_HEALTH
	jsr cmdClass0
	bne dhRetry
	jsr hbCheck
	beq dhDone
dhRetry
	ldx hbTry
	dex
	bne dhTry
	lda hbBad
	cmp #255
	beq dhDone
	inc hbBad
dhDone
	rts

; A = 0 if the block arrived whole. The magic says a block arrived at all;
; the XOR catches a wrong byte and the sum catches a transposition the XOR
; is blind to.
hbCheck
	lda healthBuf+HB_MAGIC
	cmp #$47			; 'G'
	bne hcBad
	lda healthBuf+HB_MAGIC+1
	cmp #$36			; '6'
	bne hcBad
	lda #0
	sta hbXor
	sta hbSum
	ldx #0
hcSum
	lda healthBuf,x
	eor hbXor
	sta hbXor
	clc
	lda hbSum
	adc healthBuf,x
	sta hbSum
	inx
	cpx #HB_MAGIC+2
	bne hcSum
	lda hbXor
	cmp healthBuf+HB_MAGIC+2
	bne hcBad
	lda hbSum
	cmp healthBuf+HB_MAGIC+3
	bne hcBad
	lda #0
	rts
hcBad
	lda #$ff
	rts

; A = the core temperature in whole degrees. The block carries tenths, and
; a reader looking at a photograph wants 62 and not 026A.
hbTempC
	lda healthBuf+HB_TEMP
	sta tmpLo
	lda healthBuf+HB_TEMP+1
	sta tmpHi
	ldx #0
htLoop
	lda tmpLo
	sec
	sbc #10
	tay
	lda tmpHi
	sbc #0
	bcc htDone			; less than ten tenths left
	sty tmpLo
	sta tmpHi
	inx
	cpx #255
	bne htLoop
htDone
	txa
	rts

; ======================================================================
; The millisecond clock. See CIA2_* above for why it is a CIA timer pair
; and not the jiffy counter.
; ======================================================================

; Start timer A on a 1ms period and timer B counting its underflows. Both
; continuous, both force-loaded from their latches by bit 4, neither
; enabled as an interrupt source.
clkInit
	lda #<CLK_PERIOD
	sta CIA2_TALO
	lda #>CLK_PERIOD
	sta CIA2_TAHI
	lda #$ff
	sta CIA2_TBLO
	sta CIA2_TBHI
	lda #%00010001			; force load, continuous, start
	sta CIA2_CRA
	lda #%01010001			; + count timer A underflows
	sta CIA2_CRB
	; Both marks start here, so the first frame measures from the first
	; frame and not from whenever the counter happened to be reset.
	jsr clkNow
	lda msNow
	sta msMark
	sta msFrameMark
	lda msNow+1
	sta msMark+1
	sta msFrameMark+1
	rts

; msNow = timer B, read whole. High, low, high again: the counter ticks
; once a millisecond, so a read that straddles a borrow is rare and cheap
; to reject, and the alternative -- accepting it -- shows up as a 256ms
; frame that never happened.
clkNow
	lda CIA2_TBHI
	sta msNow+1
	lda CIA2_TBLO
	sta msNow
	lda CIA2_TBHI
	cmp msNow+1
	bne clkNow
	rts

; X = slot. Stores (now - msMark) in it, keeps the running maximum, and
; moves the mark to now, so consecutive calls carve a frame into phases
; with nothing unaccounted between them. Clamped to 255: a phase that long
; is off the scale either way, and three digits is what fits on the row.
clkLap
	lda teleOn			; only the rows read the laps; clkFrame
	bne +				; re-marks msMark each frame regardless
	rts
+	jsr clkNow
	lda msMark			; counts down
	sec
	sbc msNow
	sta clkTmp
	lda msMark+1
	sbc msNow+1
	beq +
	lda #$ff
	sta clkTmp
+
	lda msNow
	sta msMark
	lda msNow+1
	sta msMark+1
	lda clkTmp
	sta msCur,x
	cmp msMax,x
	bcc +
	sta msMax,x
+
	rts

; The frame limiter: a frame never takes less than PACE_MS. The game's
; pace no longer depends on it -- motion is scaled by dt and everything that
; counts runs on the game tick (tickFrame) -- so this only keeps a turbo CPU
; (the C64U's 64MHz mode, or any accelerator) from spinning faster than
; HDMI's 60 Hz can show, and keeps dtMs above DT_MIN. The CIAs keep their
; 1MHz clock in turbo, so this wait is in real milliseconds. At 1MHz a
; frame is already longer and the wait falls straight through. Only the
; main loop paces; clkFrame's other callers just re-mark.
PACE_MS = 16
	.cerror PACE_MS < DT_MIN, "a paced frame must measure at least DT_MIN"
clkPace
	jsr clkNow
	lda msFrameMark
	sec
	sbc msNow
	tax
	lda msFrameMark+1
	sbc msNow+1
	bne +				; 256ms or more: long past the limit
	cpx #PACE_MS
	bcc clkPace
+	rts

; The frame period, measured start to start, and the one reading here that
; is not a phase: it includes whatever the loop spends outside the four
; phases as well. Re-marks BOTH clocks, so the first phase of the new frame
; starts where the period does.
clkFrame
	jsr clkNow
	lda msFrameMark
	sec
	sbc msNow
	sta clkTmp
	lda msFrameMark+1
	sbc msNow+1
	beq +
	lda #$ff
	sta clkTmp
+
	lda msNow
	sta msMark
	sta msFrameMark
	lda msNow+1
	sta msMark+1
	sta msFrameMark+1
	ldx #MS_PER
	lda clkTmp
	sta msCur,x
	cmp msMax,x
	bcc +
	sta msMax,x
+
	cmp msPerMin
	bcs +
	sta msPerMin
+
	; fall through

; The frame's motion scale, from the period just measured (clkTmp). Every
; per-frame step the frame will take is computed here, once.
dtFrame
	lda clkTmp
	cmp #DT_MIN
	bcs +
	lda #DT_MIN
+	cmp #DT_MAX+1
	bcc +
	lda #DT_MAX
+	sta dtMs
	jsr tickFrame
	ldx dtMs
	lda walkT,x
	sta walkStep
	lda runT,x
	sta runStep
	stx doorStep			; DOOR_STEP * dt / DT_REF = dt
	lda gravLoT,x
	sta gravD
	lda gravHiT,x
	sta gravD+1
	txa
	.cerror DOOR_STEP != DT_REF || PITCH_STEP != 4 || TURN != 512, "dtFrame's shortcuts"
	lsr a
	lsr a
	lsr a
	sta pitchStep			; PITCH_STEP * dt / 32 = dt / 8
	lda #0
	sta turnD+1
	txa
	.rept 4
	asl a				; TURN * dt / 32 = dt * 16
	rol turnD+1
	.endrept
	sta turnD
	rts

; The game tick. Logic that counts FRAMES -- door waits, weapon cooldowns,
; the AI's think cadence, actor animation, the message and flash timers,
; the void counter -- runs only on a tick frame, once per DT_REF ms of real
; time, and sees a dt of DT_REF (tickLogic). Everything a frame SHOWS --
; the player's move, the camera, water, the render -- runs every frame on
; the real dtMs. So the render rate is free (60 Hz on a fast enough turbo)
; and the game's pace is bound to time at any clock.
;
; A frame of DT_REF or longer is a tick of its own length, exactly as every
; frame was before the tick existed: at 1 MHz, and in runsim at
; --frame-ms=32, every frame ticks and nothing changes. Shorter frames add
; up in tickAcc until DT_REF is due.
tickFrame
	lda dtMs
	cmp #DT_REF
	bcc +
	sta tickDt			; a slow frame: one tick, its own length
	lda #1
	sta tick
	rts
+	adc tickAcc			; (C clear)
	cmp #DT_REF
	bcc +
	sbc #DT_REF			; (C set)
	sta tickAcc
	lda #DT_REF
	sta tickDt
	lda #1
	sta tick
	rts
+	sta tickAcc
	lda #0
	sta tick
	rts

; On a tick frame, after the player's own move: the tick's dt replaces the
; frame's for everything logic scales (dtMul, the doors).
tickLogic
	lda tickDt
	sta dtMs
	sta doorStep
	rts

; The per-frame steps by dtMs, rounded down as dtMul would.
walkT	.for i = 0, i <= DT_MAX, i += 1
	.byte WALK * i / DT_REF
	.next
runT	.for i = 0, i <= DT_MAX, i += 1
	.byte WALK * 2 * i / DT_REF
	.next
gravLoT	.for i = 0, i <= DT_MAX, i += 1
	.byte <( GRAV * i / DT_REF )
	.next
gravHiT	.for i = 0, i <= DT_MAX, i += 1
	.byte >( GRAV * i / DT_REF )
	.next

; A = A * dtMs / DT_REF, for a step no bigger than DT_MAX lets fit a byte
; (the run step, 136, is the largest). Preserves X.
dtMul
	stx dtX
	ldy dtMs
	jsr mul8
	lda m8L
	.cerror DT_REF != 32, "the five shifts below are / DT_REF"
	.rept 5
	lsr m8H
	ror a
	.endrept
	ldx dtX
	rts

; dtR (signed 24-bit) = mA (signed 16-bit) * dtMs / DT_REF. mulTrig takes a
; scale of at most 256, so it is given dtMs * 4 (<= 240) and the product is
; doubled back; every value scaled here is even, so at DT_REF it is exact.
; Destroys mA. Preserves X.
dtMulS
	stx dtX
	lda dtMs
	asl a
	asl a
	sta mS
	lda #0
	sta mS+1
	jsr mulTrig			; mR = mA * dtMs / 64
	lda mR+1
	and #$80
	beq +
	lda #$ff
+	sta dtR+2
	lda mR
	asl a
	sta dtR
	lda mR+1
	rol a
	sta dtR+1
	rol dtR+2
	ldx dtX
	rts

; ======================================================================
; Staging an argument block twice. Run 40 flickered the rest of E1M1 through
; the walls about once a second, and it was not the renderer: a register
; write the polling loop does not sample leaves the register holding the
; PREVIOUS command's byte, and before sendCamera that is a mover's position.
; One dropped camera byte put the eye 33 or 65 units away for a frame; one
; dropped ID byte moved a level node onto the camera, or a door onto another
; door's id, until a refresh put it back (runsim --bus-fault=drop:400 shows
; all three in 1500 frames). The retained scene cannot tell those writes
; from real ones, so the only defence is not to lose them: every byte goes
; to its register twice, a whole block apart, so a lost write is repaired
; by the second pass instead of being believed.
;
; Run 41 then showed what double-writing cannot cover: a write sampled with
; a bit flipped is believed by the second pass just as well. runsim
; --bus-fault=data:300 still put the camera 4, 8 or 128 units off for a
; frame and a door 8 units off until the refresh. So the block now carries
; its own check (GPU64_KEY_CHECKED, gpu64_api.h): ARG15 = $D0|n and ARG14 =
; the XOR of that key, CMD_HI, CMD_LO, ID_LO, ID_HI and ARG0..n-1. The Pi
; refuses a block that does not add up with BAD_ARGS and executes nothing,
; and every command sent this way is absolute, so it is simply sent again.
; A firmware older than the check ignores the key and runs it unchecked.
;
; The check made the second pass redundant, so the block now goes once
; (section 75): a dropped or flipped ARG/ID byte fails the XOR, and a
; dropped ARG15 runs the block unchecked, which strict mode refuses once
; the first checked SCENE_COMMIT has latched it. Either way it is BAD_ARGS
; and sent again. The second pass was over 40% of every frame's writes.
;
; stId and stBuf hold the block; A = the class 1 opcode, X = the last ARG
; index, or $FF for a command with no arguments. Sends it, and returns like cmd1: ERRCODE in A, Z set on OK.
; Destroys A, X, Y.
; ======================================================================
stage2
	sta stOp
	stx stLast
	inx
	txa
	ora #KEY_CHECKED
	sta stKey
	eor #1				; CMD_HI: this is always class 1
	eor stOp
	eor stId
	eor stId+1
	ldx stLast
	bmi +				; X = $FF: no arguments at all
-	eor stBuf,x
	dex
	bpl -
+	sta stChk
	lda #CHK_TRIES
	sta stTry
st2Send
	ldy #1				; one pass: the check replaced the second
st2Pass
	lda stId
	sta ID_LO
	lda stId+1
	sta ID_HI
	ldx stLast
	bmi +
-	lda stBuf,x
	sta ARG,x
	dex
	bpl -
+	lda stChk
	sta ARG+14
	lda stKey
	sta ARG+15
	dey
	bne st2Pass
	lda stOp
	jsr cmd1
	cmp #ERR_BAD_ARGS
	bne st2Done
	inc chkRetry
	bne +
	inc chkRetry+1
+	dec stTry
	bne st2Send
	lda #ERR_BAD_ARGS
st2Done
	cmp #0
	rts

; ======================================================================
; One class 1 command. The ERRCODE range test and the eight retries are the
; run 27 lesson: an unreadable answer is NOT a failed command, and a single
; garbage byte turned into a control-flow decision cost a whole bench run.
; ======================================================================
;
; cmdClass0 is the same machinery aimed at class 0, for the one class 0 opcode
; this program sends. It shares every retry with cmd1 because the argument for
; them is about the register window, not about the class. (Not "cmd0": that is
; a macro in gpu64.inc, and a label shadowing it assembles into a very
; confusing error somewhere else entirely.)
cmdClass0
	ldx #0
	stx CMD_HI
	jsr cEntry
	; CMD_HI straight back to 1. Left at 0, every ARG byte the next class 1
	; command stages goes out under class 0, and one of $0B that the bus
	; turns into a CMD_LO write is a FULL_RESET: every page black, the
	; loop stopped (2026-09-28 bench, "dark like powered off").
	ldx #1
	stx CMD_HI
	cmp #0
	rts
cmd1
	ldx #1
	stx CMD_HI
cEntry
	sta CMD_LO
	lda ERRCODE
	cmp #$0d			; the valid codes run $00-$0C
	bcc c1Done
	ldx #7
c1ReRead
	lda ERRCODE
	cmp #$0d
	bcc c1Done
	dex
	bne c1ReRead
	inc errBad
	bne +
	inc errBad+1
+	lda #0				; report OK: the reply was lost, not the command
c1Done
	; Flags from the RETURN VALUE, not from the range test. Without this the
	; Z flag a caller branches on is whatever `cmp #$0d` left behind -- which
	; for a perfectly good ERRCODE of 0 is Z CLEAR, so every `beq` on a
	; successful command falls through into its own error path. Caught on a
	; PC before the bench, which is what tools/prgsim is for.
	cmp #0
	rts

; The same with a sequence number, which is the only detector there is for
; a CMD_LO or SEQ write the polling loop did not sample. Leaves the answer
; in seqErr and the verdict in seqMiss (0 = the command was seen).
cmdSeq1
	sta seqOp
	inc dispCt
	bne +
	inc dispCt+1
	bne +
	inc dispCt+2
+
	; Never 0 and never $FF: 0 would let an unwritten SEQACK read as a
	; match, $FF would let a dropped READ of SEQACK -- which returns a
	; floating $FF -- read as one.
	inc seqCounter
	lda seqCounter
	beq csWrap
	cmp #$ff
	bne csSeqOk
csWrap	lda #1
	sta seqCounter
csSeqOk
	lda seqCounter
	sta SEQ
	lda seqOp
	jsr cmd1
	sta seqErr

	lda SEQACK
	cmp seqCounter
	beq csOk
	; Read it again. SEQACK is a plain latch and the command finished long
	; before the first read returned, so a second read that agrees proves
	; the READ was the failure and the firmware was right all along.
	lda SEQACK
	cmp seqCounter
	beq csOk
	inc missed
	bne +
	inc missed+1
+	lda #1
	sta seqMiss
	rts
csOk
	lda #0
	sta seqMiss
	rts

; ======================================================================
; The display. Four rows, all of them printed every frame -- run 18 froze
; at frame 256 and returned nothing because its rows were printed only at
; finish, and a blank row is not a zero.

; ======================================================================
; The display. Four rows, all of them printed every frame -- run 18 froze
; at frame 256 and returned nothing because its rows were printed only at
; finish, and a blank row is not a zero.
; ======================================================================
; The C64-screen telemetry in slices, one every other frame: drawn whole it
; was ~40000 cycles in one frame, a hitch every eighth frame. showFrame still
; draws all of it, for finish.
; F5 or start-up: rows 2-24 blanked, and while telemetry is off a line
; saying how to bring it back. Rows 0-1 (title, SETUP) stay.
teleShown
	ldx #0
	lda #$20
-	sta SCREEN+80,x
	sta SCREEN+80+$100,x
	sta SCREEN+80+$200,x
	inx
	bne -
-	sta SCREEN+80+$300,x		; the last 920 - 768 = 152
	inx
	cpx #152
	bne -
	lda teleOn
	bne +
	lda #2
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "TELEMETRY OFF - F5 SHOWS IT"
	.byte 0
+	rts

showSlice
	dec dispWait
	bne ssRts
	lda #DISP_EVERY
	sta dispWait
	ldx dispSlice
	inx
	cpx #SHOW_N
	bcc +
	ldx #0
+	stx dispSlice
	lda showHi,x
	pha
	lda showLo,x
	pha
ssRts
	rts				; to the slice, which returns to our caller

showFrame
	ldx #0
-	stx dispSlice
	lda #>( sfNext - 1 )
	pha
	lda #<( sfNext - 1 )
	pha
	lda showHi,x
	pha
	lda showLo,x
	pha
	rts
sfNext
	ldx dispSlice
	inx
	cpx #SHOW_N
	bne -
	rts

SHOW_TAB = [showS0, showS1, showS2, showS3, showS4, showS5, showS6, showS7, showWorld, showCombat, showAi, showEnv]
SHOW_N = len(SHOW_TAB)
showLo	.byte <( SHOW_TAB - 1 )
showHi	.byte >( SHOW_TAB - 1 )

showS0
	; Row 4: where this program believes the camera is. The three integer
	; world coordinates and the top byte of the heading, which is also the
	; sine table index the step was taken with.
	;
	; All three coordinates now come back from CLIP_MOVE, so Y is no longer
	; the fixed reference an earlier version of this program could check a
	; corrupted register write against -- row 13 and the block's own
	; checksum are what watch the move instead. What Y still says at a
	; glance is whether the player is standing on something: in E1M1's
	; first room the eye sits near 0070, and a Y falling without bound is
	; the floor trace failing. Printed as the integer part only: bench
	; telemetry is read off a photograph of a CRT, and a reader who has to
	; subtract two numbers to reach a verdict will not.
	lda #4
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "CAM X"
	.byte 0
	lda cam+CAM_X+3
	jsr dmHexA
	lda cam+CAM_X+2
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " Y"
	.byte 0
	lda cam+CAM_Y+3
	jsr dmHexA
	lda cam+CAM_Y+2
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " Z"
	.byte 0
	lda cam+CAM_Z+3
	jsr dmHexA
	lda cam+CAM_Z+2
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " YAW"
	.byte 0
	lda camYaw+1
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " P"
	.byte 0
	lda posErr
	jsr dmHexA

	lda #5
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "FRAMES  "
	.byte 0
	lda frames+1
	jsr dmHexA
	lda frames
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text "  OK "
	.byte 0
	lda okFrames+1
	jsr dmHexA
	lda okFrames
	jsr dmHexA
	rts

showS1

	lda #6
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "CMT  07 "
	.byte 0
	lda e07+1
	jsr dmHexA
	lda e07
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text "  06 "
	.byte 0
	lda e06+1
	jsr dmHexA
	lda e06
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text "  XX "
	.byte 0
	lda eXX+1
	jsr dmHexA
	lda eXX
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " "
	.byte 0
	lda xxCode
	jsr dmHexA

	lda #7
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "MISSED  "
	.byte 0
	lda missed+1
	jsr dmHexA
	lda missed
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text "  DISP "
	.byte 0
	lda dispCt+2
	jsr dmHexA
	lda dispCt+1
	jsr dmHexA
	lda dispCt
	jsr dmHexA
	rts

showS2

	lda #8
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "STALLS  "
	.byte 0
	lda stalls+1
	jsr dmHexA
	lda stalls
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text "  AT "
	.byte 0
	lda stallAt+1
	jsr dmHexA
	lda stallAt
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text "  ERRBAD "
	.byte 0
	lda errBad+1
	jsr dmHexA
	lda errBad
	jsr dmHexA

	; Rows 9 and 10: where a frame's time goes, in milliseconds off CIA2.
	; Decimal, because the question these answer is "how many frames a
	; second" and the reader has a photograph, not a calculator.
	;
	; Row 9 is the frame period: 1000/CUR is the rate the player is
	; actually getting, and MIN against MAX is the steadiness they
	; complained about. Row 10 splits the worst frame into its phases, so
	; a large period names its own cause -- WAIT is core 1 rendering,
	; CMT is core 1 rendering with the C64 halted, CAM is the command
	; path, DISP is these two rows costing more than they are worth.
	;
	; The DISP figure is one frame behind by construction: it is measured
	; after this routine returns.
	lda #9
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "MS/FRAME  CUR "
	.byte 0
	lda msCur+MS_PER
	jsr dmDecA
	jsr dmText
	.text "  MIN "
	.byte 0
	lda msPerMin
	jsr dmDecA
	jsr dmText
	.text "  MAX "
	.byte 0
	lda msMax+MS_PER
	jsr dmDecA
	rts

showS3

	lda #10
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "MS MAX  CAM "
	.byte 0
	lda msMax+MS_CAM
	jsr dmDecA
	jsr dmText
	.text " WAIT "
	.byte 0
	lda msMax+MS_WAIT
	jsr dmDecA
	jsr dmText
	.text " CMT "
	.byte 0
	lda msMax+MS_CMT
	jsr dmDecA
	jsr dmText
	.text " D "
	.byte 0
	lda msMax+MS_DISP
	jsr dmDecA

	; Rows 11 and 12: the Pi's own account of itself, resampled every
	; HB_EVERY frames.
	;
	; PWR is the throttle word latched since this program started, in hex
	; because it is a bit field: 0000 is the healthy reading and bit 0 or
	; bit 16 set is a power verdict, not a hint. BAD counts health samples
	; that failed to verify -- without it a frozen row reads as a Pi whose
	; numbers stopped changing.
	;
	; FLIP is what the Pi thinks it presented and OK is what this program
	; thinks it asked for, both as of the same sample. They are the two
	; ends of one count, and only one of them is the picture. SLOW and DTO
	; are the flip path's two pathologies (Source/Firmware/gpu64_flip.h);
	; both should be 0000.
	lda #11
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "PWR "
	.byte 0
	lda healthBuf+HB_PWR+1
	jsr dmHexA
	lda healthBuf+HB_PWR
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text "  HOT "
	.byte 0
	jsr hbTempC
	jsr dmDecA
	jsr dmText
	.text "  HB "
	.byte 0
	lda msMax+MS_HB
	jsr dmDecA
	jsr dmText
	.text "  BAD "
	.byte 0
	lda hbBad
	jsr dmDecA
	rts

showS4

	lda #12
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "FLIP "
	.byte 0
	lda healthBuf+HB_FLIPS+1
	jsr dmHexA
	lda healthBuf+HB_FLIPS
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " OK "
	.byte 0
	lda hbOkAt+1
	jsr dmHexA
	lda hbOkAt
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " SLOW "
	.byte 0
	lda healthBuf+HB_SLOW+1
	jsr dmHexA
	lda healthBuf+HB_SLOW
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " DTO "
	.byte 0
	lda healthBuf+HB_DTO+1
	jsr dmHexA
	lda healthBuf+HB_DTO
	jsr dmHexA

	; Row 13: the collision answer, and then four separate ways it can go
	; wrong. F 01 is standing on ground, which is what walking looks like;
	; 02 means the step hit a wall and slid along it; 08 means it climbed
	; something; 10 means the eye started inside solid and the move was
	; refused. FR is how much of the requested step was actually covered,
	; out of FF. E is the LAST ERRCODE, a diagnostic only -- see clipMove.
	;
	; The four counters are deliberately not one number. Run 37 printed a
	; single BAD of 1072 frames in 1753 and that number could not say which
	; direction the bytes were being lost in, which is the only thing worth
	; knowing:
	;   N  attempts whose ERRCODE came back non-zero. The Pi rejected the
	;      INPUT block, so a byte we wrote never arrived.
	;   M  attempts whose output magic was wrong. The Pi's writeback never
	;      landed at all, or landed somewhere else.
	;   S  attempts whose output checksum was wrong but magic was right.
	;      The writeback landed and arrived damaged.
	;   W  answers that passed magic AND checksum and were still refused,
	;      because they put the player further away than the step asked
	;      for. This is the bus getting past both channel checks, and one
	;      of these uncaught is a permanently wrong position (run 36).
	; N M S all retry, so they cost a frame's precision, not the frame.
	lda #13
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "CLIP F"
	.byte 0
	lda clipFlags
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " FR"
	.byte 0
	lda clipFrac
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " E"
	.byte 0
	lda clipErr
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " N"
	.byte 0
	lda clipN+1
	jsr dmHexA
	lda clipN
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " M"
	.byte 0
	lda clipM+1
	jsr dmHexA
	lda clipM
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " S"
	.byte 0
	lda clipS+1
	jsr dmHexA
	lda clipS
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " W"
	.byte 0
	lda clipWild
	jsr dmHexA
	rts

showS5

	; Rows 14-16: the game. These are what separate this program from the
	; level walk-through it grew out of.
	;
	; Row 14 is the frame's work: how many movers and trigger volumes the
	; scan found, how many of the movers were handed to this frame's trace
	; (SEL, never more than 16), how many are mid-animation, and what the
	; whole of the game logic costs in milliseconds. MS is the honest
	; answer to "can a 6502 run this": if it is small, the C64 is not the
	; reason a frame is slow.
	;
	; Row 15 is causation, which is the row to read when a door will not
	; open. OPENED counts CLOSED->OPENING transitions since start-up, so
	; walking into a door and seeing it not move is one reading and seeing
	; the count rise anyway is a different bug entirely. FIRED is the last
	; targetname id put on the queue and NODE the last scene node a
	; SET_POSITION was aimed at -- a FIRED that changes with a NODE that
	; does not means the wiring found nothing to open.
	;
	; Row 16 is the scan, printed because everything above it is downstream
	; of it: ENTS is the level's entity count as the Pi reports it, BAD the
	; records that failed magic-and-checksum three times running, and DROP
	; the ones that arrived fine and had nowhere to go because a table was
	; full. A nonzero DROP on E1M1 means MAX_MOV or MAX_TRG is too small
	; for this level, and is a different complaint from BAD.
	lda #14
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "GAME  MOV "
	.byte 0
	lda nMov
	jsr dmDecA
	jsr dmText
	.text " TRG "
	.byte 0
	lda nTrg
	jsr dmDecA
	jsr dmText
	.text " SEL "
	.byte 0
	lda selCnt
	jsr dmDecA
	jsr dmText
	.text " MS "
	.byte 0
	lda msMax+MS_GAME
	jsr dmDecA

	lda #15
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "OPEN "
	.byte 0
	lda openCt+1
	jsr dmHexA
	lda openCt
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " ANI "
	.byte 0
	lda aniCt
	jsr dmDecA
	jsr dmText
	.text " FIRE "
	.byte 0
	lda lastFireH
	jsr dmHexA
	lda lastFireL
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " NODE "
	.byte 0
	lda lastNodeH
	jsr dmHexA
	lda lastNodeL
	jsr dmHexA
	rts

showS6

	lda #16
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "ENTS "
	.byte 0
	lda entTot+1
	jsr dmHexA
	lda entTot
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " BAD "
	.byte 0
	lda entBad
	jsr dmDecA
	jsr dmText
	.text " DROP "
	.byte 0
	lda entDrop
	jsr dmDecA
	jsr dmText
	.text " PE "
	.byte 0
	lda movErr
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " CK"
	.byte 0
	lda chkRetry+1
	jsr dmHexA
	lda chkRetry
	jsr dmHexA

	; Row 17: the view the keys have set; NODES is how long the level's
	; node run turned out to be, FIX how many times LEVEL_NODE found one
	; damaged and put it back.
	lda #17
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "VIEW FOV "
	.byte 0
	ldx fovIx
	lda fovDegTab,x
	jsr dmDecA
	jsr dmText
	.text " PIT "
	.byte 0
	lda camPitch
	bmi sfPitNeg
	jsr dmText
	.text "+"
	.byte 0
	lda camPitch
	jmp sfPit
sfPitNeg
	jsr dmText
	.text "-"
	.byte 0
	lda #0
	sec
	sbc camPitch
sfPit
	jsr dmDecA
	jsr dmText
	.text " NODES "
	.byte 0
	lda lnCount
	jsr dmDecA
	jsr dmText
	.text " FIX "
	.byte 0
	lda lnFix
	jsr dmDecA
	rts

showS7

	; Row 18: the refresh rings. PAL is how many times LEVEL_PALETTE found
	; the palette damaged and put it back, LAST how many entries the most
	; recent repair touched -- a nonzero PAL is a phantom that would have
	; been permanent before this ring existed. CAM is how many times the
	; camera had stopped being a camera and was made one again.
	lda #18
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "RING ERR "
	.byte 0
	lda rgErrs
	jsr dmDecA
	jsr dmText
	.text " PAL "
	.byte 0
	lda palFixes+1
	jsr dmHexA
	lda palFixes
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " LAST "
	.byte 0
	lda palLast
	jsr dmDecA
	jsr dmText
	.text " CAM "
	.byte 0
	lda camFix
	jsr dmDecA

	; Row 19: the player's own body. STUCK is how many answers started or
	; ended inside solid and put the player back where they last stood;
	; VOID how many falls ran past VOID_FRAMES and did the same. Either
	; one nonzero is a bad answer that got past the checksum AND the bound
	; and would have been the rest of the session (run 39). JMP counts
	; jumps, G is 1 while standing. D is DRY: frames in a row, now and at
	; worst, in which no CLIP_MOVE answer was taken -- see cmDry.
	lda #19
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "BODY JMP "
	.byte 0
	lda jumps
	jsr dmDecA
	jsr dmText
	.text " STK "
	.byte 0
	lda clipStuck
	jsr dmDecA
	jsr dmText
	.text " VD "
	.byte 0
	lda clipVoid
	jsr dmDecA
	jsr dmText
	.text " G"
	.byte 0
	lda onGround
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " D"
	.byte 0
	lda clipDry
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text "/"
	.byte 0
	lda clipDryMax
	jsr dmHexA
	rts

fovDegTab
	.for i = 0, i < FOV_N, i += 1
	.byte FOV_DEG0 + i * FOV_STEP
	.next

; The load's verdict, which is the whole reading if the level never built.
; Prints the ERRCODE and the item number RESULT carries -- the loader stamps
; the failing item there, so a failure names its address and not just its
; kind.
showLoadErr
	lda #2
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "LOAD    FAILED ERR "
	.byte 0
	lda loadErr
	ora stepErr
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " AT PCT "
	.byte 0
	lda stepPct
	jsr dmHexA
	jsr dmText
	.text " ITEM "
	.byte 0
	lda RESULT
	jsr dmHexA

	lda #3
	jsr dmRow
	jsr dmText
	.text "04 USUALLY MEANS NO RAD/LEVEL.G64LEV"
	.byte 0
	rts

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
vpErr		.byte 0
perErr		.byte 0
resErr		.byte 0
camErr		.byte 0
loopErr		.byte 0
loadErr		.byte 0
lvSlot		.byte 0		; the Pi's level slot: n-1 for E1Mn
stepErr		.byte 0
stepPct		.byte 0
stepCt		.word 0
stopTry		.byte 0

frames		.word 0
okFrames	.word 0
e06		.word 0
e07		.word 0
eXX		.word 0
xxCode		.byte 0
missed		.word 0
errBad		.word 0
dispCt		.byte 0, 0, 0
stalls		.word 0
stallAt		.word 0
waitCt		.word 0
wrMs		.byte 0
wrPumps		.word 0

; The clock's state. msCur/msMax are indexed by the MS_* slots.
msNow		.word 0
msMark		.word 0
msFrameMark	.word 0
clkTmp		.byte 0
msCur		.fill MS_SLOTS, 0
teleOn		.byte 0
teleHold	.byte 0
msMax		.fill MS_SLOTS, 0
msPerMin	.byte $ff
dispWait	.byte 1
dispSlice	.byte 0

hbWait		.byte 1
hbOkAt		.word 0
hbTry		.byte 0
hbBad		.byte 0
hbXor		.byte 0
hbSum		.byte 0
tmpLo		.byte 0
tmpHi		.byte 0

seqCounter	.byte 0
seqOp		.byte 0
seqErr		.byte 0
seqMiss		.byte 0
keyB		.byte 0
keyC		.byte 0
keyD		.byte 0
stepLo		.byte 0
bmChk		.byte 0
dtMs		.byte DT_REF
tick		.byte 1			; this frame is a game tick (tickFrame)
tickAcc		.byte 0			; ms toward the next tick, < DT_REF
tickDt		.byte DT_REF		; the tick's dt
dtX		.byte 0
dtR		.fill 3, 0
walkStep	.byte WALK
runStep		.byte WALK * 2
doorStep	.byte DOOR_STEP
pitchStep	.byte PITCH_STEP
turnD		.word TURN
gravD		.word GRAV

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; The camera this program owns. Twelve bytes in exactly the order
; SET_POSITION reads them -- x, y, z as signed 16.16 world units -- so
; sending it is a copy loop and never a rearrangement.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
cam		.fill 12, 0
camYaw		.word 0

; Where the Pi's two readbacks land: GET_HEALTH's block and the starting
; pose GET_TRANSFORM hands back. Adjacent, and in this order, because one
; SET_DMA_WINDOW covers both -- see DMA_LEN. A dropped ARG byte can still
; corrupt the destination address; what it can no longer do is aim the
; writeback at this program's code.
healthBuf	.fill HB_LEN, 0
camBuf		.fill CAM_BUF_LEN, 0
clipBlk		.fill CLIP_LEN, 0
bmStale		.byte 1

; And the entity record, which is a readback too: it has to be inside the
; same window, so it is declared here beside the others and not down with
; the rest of the game's state.
entBlk		.fill ENT_LEN, 0

; The WORLD_TICK block (gpu64_game_world.inc): the answers are written into
; its tail, so it is a readback and lives in the window too.
wtBlk		.fill WT_LEN, 0

; The displacement this frame asks CLIP_MOVE for: twelve bytes in the same
; x, y, z 16.16 order as cam, because camAdd accumulates into it with the
; same CAM_X/CAM_Y/CAM_Z offsets.
mov		.fill 12, 0

; CLIP_MOVE's last answer, kept for row 13. clipCont and clipBumps are read
; out of the block but no longer printed: row 13 has to fit in 40 columns and
; the four rejection counters below earned those columns off run 37.
clipFlags	.byte 0
clipCont	.byte 0
clipFrac	.byte 0
clipBumps	.byte 0
clipErr		.byte 0

; Why the answer was refused, split three ways, because the three have
; nothing to do with each other. N is a non-zero ERRCODE: the Pi rejected
; what we sent, or the ERRCODE read itself was garbled. M is a bad output
; magic: the writeback never landed and the poison is still there. S is a
; bad output checksum: the writeback landed corrupted. One lumped counter
; said 61% of frames failed and could not say which way the bytes were
; going.
clipN		.word 0
clipM		.word 0
clipS		.word 0
cmTry		.byte 0

; clipWild counts the answers that passed magic and checksum and were still
; refused, because they put the eye further from the start than the step
; asked for. Distinct from N/M/S on purpose: those three are the bus being
; caught by a channel check, clipWild is the bus getting past both of them
; and being caught by what the answer MEANS instead.
clipWild	.byte 0
cbD0		.byte 0
cbD1		.byte 0
cbM0		.byte 0
cbM1		.byte 0
cbD2		.byte 0

; The jump and the recovery. velY is the vertical step in 16.16's low word,
; signed; VEL_MIN while standing. lastGood is the eye where the player last
; stood, which a stuck or lost player is put back to. clipStuck counts
; answers that started or ended in solid, clipVoid falls that ran out of
; VOID_FRAMES -- both saturate, both on row 19.
velY		.word VEL_MIN
onGround	.byte 0
jumping		.byte 0
spcHold		.byte 0
airFrames	.byte 0
jumps		.byte 0
clipStuck	.byte 0
clipVoid	.byte 0
stuckRun	.byte 0
lastGood	.fill 12

; camAdd's workspace.
mLo		.byte 0
mHi		.byte 0
mult		.byte 0
prod0		.byte 0
prod1		.byte 0
prod2		.byte 0
dirNeg		.byte 0
caNeg		.byte 0
camOff		.byte 0

; Verdicts. posErr is the per-frame camera send and belongs on row 4;
; getErr and the fence belong to setup and are printed on row 3.
posErr		.byte 0
getErr		.byte 0
getTry		.byte 0
fenceErr	.byte 0
fenceTry	.byte 0
fenceEcho	.byte 0

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; The game's own state. Every table is a set of PARALLEL byte arrays
; indexed by the mover or trigger number, and not an array of structures:
; a structure of this width needs a 16-bit offset and a multiply to reach,
; where parallel arrays are one `lda table,x` each. It is more lines of
; source and far less code, and X addresses all of it directly.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
nMov		.byte 0
nTrg		.byte 0
entIdx		.word 0
entTot		.word 0
entBad		.byte 0
entDrop		.byte 0
erCount		.byte 0

; What a mover IS: its model, the run of scene nodes that draw it, and the
; two pool ids that wire it to the rest of the level.
mvModel		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvNodeL		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvNodeH		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvNodeC		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvKind		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvTnL		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvTnH		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvTgL		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvTgH		.fill MAX_MOV, 0

; What a mover is DOING: the state machine, how far along it is out of 255,
; how many frames it stays open, and whether its nodes need re-sending.
mvState		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvFrac		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvWait		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvDirty		.fill MAX_MOV, 0

; Where it is: base (the entity origin, 16.16), travel (the full
; displacement when open, 16.16) and ofs (travel * frac, recomputed only on
; the frames the fraction changes).
mvBX0		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvBX1		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvBX2		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvBX3		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvBY0		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvBY1		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvBY2		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvBY3		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvBZ0		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvBZ1		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvBZ2		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvBZ3		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvTX1		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvTX2		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvTX3		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvTY1		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvTY2		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvTY3		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvTZ1		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvTZ2		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvTZ3		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvOX0		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvOX1		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvOX2		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvOX3		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvOY0		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvOY1		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvOY2		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvOY3		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvOZ0		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvOZ1		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvOZ2		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvOZ3		.fill MAX_MOV, 0

; The closed bounding box, in fine units (1/256 world unit, signed 16-bit).
mvMnXl		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvMnXh		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvMnYl		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvMnYh		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvMnZl		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvMnZh		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvMxXl		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvMxXh		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvMxYl		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvMxYh		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvMxZl		.fill MAX_MOV, 0
mvMxZh		.fill MAX_MOV, 0

; The trigger tables are past the pause menu, under BASIC (see the end).

; This frame's selection, nearest first.
